"use strict";
// Help for the "?" button in the top right of every page (the slide-out panel).
// There is one panel for every screen and every tab, keyed by what the sidebar and the tab strips show
// (app.js: helpTopicKey).  This is plain data, not HTML: the page's security policy forbids injecting
// markup, so app.js builds the panel from these blocks.  A block is
//   "text"                     a paragraph: **bold**, `code` and *italic* are understood
//   ["h", "Heading"]           a sub-heading
//   ["ul", ["item", ...]]      a bullet list (items understand the same markup)
//   ["ol", ["step", ...]]      a numbered list, for steps done in order
//   ["note", "text"]           a highlighted note, for a warning or a thing people trip over
//   ["fields", [["Label", "what it does"], ...]]  a field list; each Label is the field's exact
//                              on-screen label (test: TestHelpCoversEveryField)
//   ["cli", "x --y\nx --z"]    command-line equivalents, shown as code
// Every panel follows the same plan: what the screen is for, what you can do on it, steps for the
// common jobs, the fields, and what is good to know.  Keep a panel's command-line block in step with
// its screen (every GUI function has a command).
const NETDIR_HELP = {
  topology: {
    title: "Topology",
    body: [
      "This page draws each gateway as a picture: the shared address an assistant connects to, the cluster nodes that serve it, its anycast addresses and the switches and firewalls it manages. It is the main place to build and change a gateway, and to work with its devices.",
      "Every change you make here is saved and applied at once. There is no Apply button.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["See at a glance whether a gateway, its nodes and its devices are working.", "Add devices to a gateway, ask them for their facts, interfaces, neighbors, configuration or a show command, and copy a device onto another gateway.", "Create, rename and delete gateways.", "Add, edit, pause and remove anycast addresses.", "Take a node out of a gateway, or put it back.", "Check that virtual MAC addresses work on your network."]],
      ["h", "The sidebar"],
      ["ul", ["Each gateway is listed under **Topology** in the sidebar. Click one to see its drawing. Right-click it to **Rename** or **Delete** it. A gateway is shown by its address, or by the **Name** you give it (right-click the circle ▸ **Edit**). The name is shared by every node in the cluster.", "**＋ New gateway** adds another gateway.", "The **dot** beside a gateway shows how the whole cluster is doing with it, whichever node you look through."]],
      ["h", "Reading the drawing"],
      ["ul", ["The **circle** is the gateway. It stands for the shared address (the *VIP*) the MCP endpoint and the GUI answer on. Hover it to see how long the cluster has served it without a break, how many nodes serve it right now, the state of each address family and **Clients**: how many different callers (a key, or the address when there is no key) sent an MCP request in the last 5 minutes, added up over the nodes that serve the gateway. The handshake counts, so an assistant that is set up but idle shows up.", "The **parallelograms** on the left are the cluster's nodes. The star marks this node, the dot the gateway controller and the triangle the cluster's primary.", "The **pills** on the right are the gateway's anycast addresses.", "The **squares** under the circle are the gateway's devices: name, address and platform. Their colour is this node's last health check: **green** answering, **amber** the API answers but refuses the login or a request, **red** unreachable, **grey** not checked yet, **dashed** disabled. Hover one for its model, OS version, uptime and when it last answered. Drag a square sideways to change the order.", "Both sides stand four to a column and spread into more columns after that. Drag an anycast pill, or a cluster node, to any slot to change the order. The order of the nodes is saved with the gateway (`node_order`, shared by the cluster)."]],
      ["h", "Right-clicking a node"],
      "Right-click one of the parallelograms to work on that node. The menu offers:",
      ["ul", ["**Controller**: this node takes the gateway controller role for every gateway. The node that held it steps down afterwards.", "**Primary**: makes this node the cluster's primary at once, the same as Cluster ▸ Promote. It is greyed out on the current primary.", "**Rename**: changes the node's host name (its operating system name and its `/etc/hosts` entry). Use letters, digits and `-`, with dots allowed. A name is letters, digits and hyphens, in up to four parts separated by dots. Every name in `/etc/hosts` that was the old host name, short or full, becomes the new one, or a `127.0.1.1` line is added. If the host name cannot be set, `/etc/hosts` is put back.", "**Pause** / **Resume**: pausing the primary hands the primary role to the most up-to-date node that is in service.", "**Restart** and **Shut down**: the whole host of that node, after you confirm. You are warned when a gateway would lose its last serving member.", "**Remove**, **Statistics** and **Open**. A node that has been removed from a gateway is not drawn on it. Right-click the gateway ▸ **Add node** to bring it back."]],
      "Hover a node for its address, its Ethernet interfaces' IPv4 and IPv6 global addresses, when it was last seen, how long its daemon has been running (Up), its version, and whether it has the primary's latest settings.",
      ["h", "Colours"],
      ["ul", ["**Green**: working. **Amber**: degraded (the gateway is still starting, or this node is not serving it yet). **Red**: down: the gateway runs but this node's MCP and web listener is not serving, so nothing can be answered here. **Grey**: not known yet, or not running here. **Dashed**: paused.", "Hover any item for its **uptime**: how long it has been online since it last came back (or how long it has been down) and how many failures it has had. It is kept in memory, so it starts again when the daemon restarts. `netdir --canvas` prints the same."]],
      ["h", "Working with the drawing"],
      ["ul", ["**Shift-click** devices to pick a run of them and **Ctrl-click** (Cmd-click on a Mac) to add or remove one, then right-click one of the picked devices: **Enable**, **Disable**, **Copy devices**, **Remove from this gateway** or **Delete devices** for all of them (the Delete key takes them off the gateway). Right-click the **circle**: *Edit*, *Add device*, *Paste device*, *Copy* ▸ *All devices* or *To all gateways*, *Ask its devices* ▸ *Facts*, *Interfaces* or *Neighbors* (opens Monitor ▸ Devices with the question asked of all of them), *Add anycast address*, *Test virtual MACs*, *Add node*, *Pause / Resume* ▸ *This node* or *All nodes*, *Delete*.", "Right-click a **device**: *Ask* ▸ *Facts*, *Interfaces*, *Ports*, *Neighbors*, *Routes*, *Firewall*, *Configuration*, *Show command* and the rest, *Network tools* ▸ *Ping*, *Traceroute*, *Scan*, *Lookup*, *Whois*, then *Disable* or *Enable* (a disabled device stays in the inventory and is never contacted; it shows grey), *Edit*, *Copy device*, *Remove from this gateway* (the Delete key does the same) and *Delete device* (out of the inventory and off every gateway).", "Right-click a **pill**: *Edit*, *Pause / Resume*, *Delete*. The Delete key also deletes the selected item.", "Right-click the empty drawing for *New gateway* and *Add anycast address*."]],
      ["h", "Step by step"],
      ["h", "Add a device"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the circle and choose **Add device**.", "Pick a device that is already in the inventory under **Device** (choose *Add ▸ Devices of the inventory*, then Shift-click or Ctrl-click to pick several at once), or keep *＋ new device* and give its name, platform, address and login. A new login needs a user name and password (Catalyst, Nexus, Arista, Aruba CX, Infoblox, Technitium, Observium) or a token (FortiGate, Palo Alto, Technitium, Observium, IP Fabric); it is named after the device.", "It appears as a square under the circle and is checked within half a minute."]],
      ["h", "Put a device on another gateway too"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the device and choose **Copy device** (or right-click the gateway's circle and choose **Copy ▸ All devices** for every device on it).", "Open the other gateway in the sidebar, right-click its circle (or the empty drawing) and choose **Paste device** (**Paste N devices**; the ones already there are left out).", "To put the devices of one gateway on every other gateway at once (each of your datacenters, say), right-click its circle and choose **Copy ▸ To all gateways**: it asks first, lists how many each gateway gets, adds only the devices a gateway lacks and removes nothing. Run it again after you change the source.", "It is the same device, not a copy: edit it on either gateway and both change. *Paste device* is greyed out, with the reason, on a gateway that has it already."]],
      ["h", "Add an anycast address"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the circle and choose **Add anycast address**.", "Type an IPv4 or IPv6 address, with no prefix length. It may be in any subnet.", "It appears as a pill on the right. Announce it to your routers on Configure ▸ Anycast, or with your own routing daemon."]],
      ["h", "Pause or resume something"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the gateway or an anycast address.", "Choose **Pause ▸ This node** (only this node stops; the others carry on) or **Pause ▸ All nodes** (every node follows).", "Resume the same way. A scope is resumed on its own: an item paused on both stays paused until both are resumed."]],
      ["h", "Take a node out of a gateway, or put it back"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the node (a parallelogram) and choose **Remove**. It gives up the gateway's address and stops answering on it.", "To put it back, right-click the gateway and choose **Add node**, then pick the node by name."]],
      ["h", "Check that virtual MAC addresses work"],
      "**Test virtual MACs** checks that replies addressed to a gateway's virtual MAC reach this node. It asks the router (or another neighbour) a question from a throwaway virtual MAC and listens for the answer, and does the same from the real MAC as a control.",
      ["ol", ["Right-click the circle and choose **Test virtual MACs**.", "Read the answer. **Delivered** means virtual MACs work here. **Not delivered** means your network drops frames sent to a virtual MAC (a VMware port group that is not promiscuous, a cloud with one MAC per interface); the dialog then offers **Use real MAC addresses**, which turns that shared option on for every node. **Inconclusive** means nothing answered even the real MAC, so nothing was proved."]],
      ["h", "Several subnets"],
      "A node runs a gateway only if its network interface has an address in the gateway's subnet (IPv4, and IPv6 when the node has a global IPv6 address). On a node that does not, the gateway shows grey (“not running here”) and starts by itself once the node is addressed there. The cluster members on that subnet serve it meanwhile.",
      ["h", "Anycast addresses"],
      "An anycast address is an extra IPv4 or IPv6 address, from **any subnet**, that every node running the gateway answers on. An assistant reaches whichever node your routers send it to.",
      ["ul", ["Anycast addresses are not checked against the interface and take no part in the election. Every node running the gateway puts them on its loopback (`lo`).", "Announce them to your routers on **Configure ▸ Anycast** (BGP or OSPF), or use your own routing daemon.", "An address is taken off `lo` (so the route is withdrawn) while this node's MCP and web listener is not serving, while the gateway is paused or stopped, and while no BGP neighbor of its family is established and OSPF does not carry it either."]],
      ["h", "Pause and resume: the rules"],
      ["ul", ["**This node** is kept in this node's own settings, so the other nodes carry on. **All nodes** is shared, so every node follows.", "A paused **gateway** releases its address and stops answering. A paused **anycast address** is not announced (the gateway keeps serving).", "The last node in service cannot be paused (Pause on the node's menu, Operate ▸ Node, `netdir --node-pause`), nor can the last node serving a gateway. A gateway can still be paused on all nodes."]],
      ["h", "Which nodes serve a gateway"],
      "Every node of the cluster serves every gateway unless it has been removed from it. A node that has just joined the cluster starts out removed from all of them, so add it where it should serve (right-click the gateway ▸ **Add node**).",
      ["ul", ["**Remove** makes a node give up the gateway's address. It is a shared setting, kept with the gateway, so you can change it from any node and it survives restarts.", "It is not a pause: a pause is for maintenance, removing a node is the gateway's list of who serves it.", "The last node serving a gateway cannot be removed (pause the gateway on all nodes instead)."]],
      ["h", "In a cluster"],
      "The drawing is shared by the whole cluster and can be edited from any node. Pausing a gateway is per node. To work on another member, pick it in the **Node** menu in the top right.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Every field of the forms on this page. Right-click the circle ▸ *Edit* (or **＋ New gateway**) for the first form.",
      ["h", "Gateway form"],
      ["fields", [["Group number", "1–255. Names the gateway: nodes that use the same number are one gateway, which is how a node joins an existing one. Chosen for you (the lowest unused number) and fixed once the gateway exists."], ["Name (optional)", "A label shown in the sidebar and on the circle instead of the address. Shared by every node in the cluster. Empty means the address is shown."], ["IPv4 address / prefix", "The address an assistant connects to (the VIP), with its prefix length, for example `10.0.0.1/24`. A node serves the gateway only if its interface has an address in that subnet. Shared by the cluster."], ["IPv6 address / prefix", "The same for IPv6, for example `2001:db8::1/64`. Leave empty for an IPv4-only gateway."], ["More IPv4 addresses", "Further shared addresses inside the VIP's subnet, separated by commas, without a prefix length (`10.0.0.2, 10.0.0.3`). They move with the gateway together with the VIP. Up to 32. Shared by the cluster."], ["More IPv6 addresses", "The same for IPv6 (`2001:db8::2`). Shared by the cluster."], ["Listening interface", "The network card this node puts the VIP on (default `eth0`). It belongs to this node only: other cluster nodes keep their own."]]],
      ["h", "Device form (Add device / Edit)"],
      ["fields", [["Add", "Add device only: *＋ new device* (the form below) or *Devices of the inventory*."], ["Devices", "Add device only, after *Devices of the inventory*: the devices of the inventory that are not on this gateway yet. Shift-click or Ctrl-click picks several."], ["Enable", "Add device only, after *Devices of the inventory*: tick it to enable the devices picked as they are added (an imported device comes in disabled). Unticked leaves each as it is."], ["Name", "Unique: up to 64 letters, digits, `.`, `-` or `_`. Renaming a device renames it on every gateway it is on."], ["Platform", "**iosxe** (Catalyst, RESTCONF), **nxos** (Nexus, NX-API), **eos** (Arista, eAPI), **aoscx** (Aruba CX, REST API), **fortios** (FortiGate, REST API), **panos** (Palo Alto firewall, XML API), **panorama**, **nios** (Infoblox, WAPI), **technitium** (DNS server), **bigip** (F5 BIG-IP, iControl REST), **meraki** (Cisco Meraki), **entra** (Microsoft Entra ID), **afc** (Aruba Fabric Composer), **proxmox** (Proxmox VE), **pbs** (Proxmox Backup Server), **pdm** (Proxmox Datacenter Manager), **observium** (Observium monitoring), **ipfabric** (IP Fabric network assurance), **aviatrix** (Aviatrix Controller), **dnsdir** (AnyName DNS Director), **ntpdir** (NTP Director) or **netdir** (MCP Network Director)."], ["Address", "The management address or host name, without a port or a scheme."], ["Port (optional)", "The API port. It starts as the default, 443 (8006 for Proxmox); the default is not written to the file."], ["Options (optional)", "Settings only some platforms take, as `key=value` pairs separated by spaces: `serial=Q2XX-XXXX-XXXX` and `org=123456` for Meraki, `tenant=contoso.onmicrosoft.com` for Entra, `api_version=v10.09` for Aruba CX, `vsys=vsys1` for PAN-OS, `node=ns1` for the directors."], ["Tags (optional)", "Separated by spaces, e.g. `site=dc1 role=core`. A request can pick them with `tag:site=dc1`."], ["Certificate", "*skip check* for a device with a self-signed certificate; *check* to require one the system trusts."], ["Login", "One of the cluster's logins (Configure ▸ Devices), or *＋ new login*."], ["User name", "A new login's user name (Catalyst, Nexus, Arista, Aruba CX, Infoblox, Technitium, Observium, IP Fabric; Entra: the application ID, or empty for a browser sign-in)."], ["Password", "A new login's password. Never shown to an MCP caller or a read-only account."], ["Token", "A new login's API token (FortiGate, Palo Alto, Technitium, Observium, IP Fabric), instead of a user name and password."], ["Command", "Show command only: one `show …` command, optionally with output filters. Nothing that writes is accepted."], ["User", "Troubleshoot user only (Entra device): a sign-in name, an object ID or a name."], ["Hours back", "Troubleshoot user only: how far back to look on the network, 1 to 168 (default 6)."]]],
      ["h", "Anycast form (Add anycast address / Edit anycast address)"],
      ["fields", [["Anycast address", "A plain IPv4 or IPv6 address of any subnet, without a prefix. It cannot be loopback, link-local, multicast or an address another gateway already uses. Every node running the gateway holds it on `lo` while it can answer."]]],
      ["cli", "netdir --canvas\nnetdir --canvas-pause gateway|anycast ... --scope node|all   netdir --canvas-pause|--canvas-resume device --device NAME\nnetdir --canvas-add gateway --vip 10.0.0.1/24 --interface eth0\nnetdir --canvas-set gateway --group 100 --vip 10.0.0.2/24\nnetdir --canvas-set gateway --group 100 --label \"Core\"   (--label - clears it)\nnetdir --canvas-add anycast --group 100 --address 203.0.113.123\nnetdir --canvas-add device --group 100 --device core-sw1   (--canvas-del device takes it off; --canvas-move device --to N reorders)\nnetdir --canvas-move anycast --group 100 --address 203.0.113.123 --to 1\nnetdir --canvas-del node --group 100 --node NODE   (--canvas-add node puts it back)\nnetdir --test-vmac --group 100\nnetdir --canvas-del gateway --group 100"],
    ],
  },
  devices: {
    title: "Devices",
    body: [
      "This page lists the switches and firewalls the gateway manages and asks them things, the same way an assistant does over MCP. Nothing is polled: a device is asked only when you press **Ask**.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["See the inventory: every device with its state (this node's last health check), platform, address and tags.", "Ask every device a target picks for its facts, interfaces, port counters, LLDP and CDP details, routes, routing protocols, firewall rules and hit counters, CPU, memory, storage and power, VLANs, anything else on the device's own API (an API catalog says what it offers), running configuration, or one show command, all at once.", "Copy or download a device's configuration.", "Ping, trace, scan, look up or whois any address, or a device's (right-click its row)."]],
      ["h", "Ask the devices"],
      ["ol", ["Type a **Target**: `all`, a device's name, `tag:site=dc1`, `platform:iosxe` or `gateway:100` (the devices on that gateway); several separated by commas. Click a device's row to fill in its name.", "Pick what to **Ask for**. For a show command, type the command.", "Press **Ask**. Every device answers on its own row or card, with how long it took, or with why it could not."]],
      ["ul", ["**Import devices (Observium, IP Fabric, Centreon, Panorama, Proxmox Datacenter Manager, Aruba Fabric Composer):** right-click the server (no *device* option) ▸ **Import** ▸ **Devices**, or **Import** ▸ **Preview** to see the list first. Every device it knows that has a driver here and is not in the inventory yet (same name or address) is added **disabled and with no login**, tagged `source=…` and its site (Observium, IP Fabric) or poller (Centreon); the others are listed as skipped, with why. Give each one a login, then enable it. `netdir --device-import SERVER [--dry-run]` does the same.", "**Configuration** is refused to a read-only account: it holds the device's own secrets.", "**Show command** takes one `show …` command, optionally with output filters (`| include`, `exclude`, `begin`, `section`, `count`, `json`). Nothing that writes is accepted. FortiGate, Aruba CX, Infoblox and Technitium APIs run no CLI commands, so they answer an error for now. Observium, Meraki, Proxmox and the directors answer their own `show` words instead (Observium: `show device NAME` gives everything about one monitored device; see the platform list under Devices). On Arista the command is run as typed; on a Palo Alto device it is an operational `show` command.", "A target that matches no device is an error, so a typo never asks fewer devices than you meant."]],
      ["h", "Change ports"],
      ["ol", ["Pick the devices in **Target**, then type the change in plain English under **Change ports**, for example `change the description for ports gi1/0/1-24 to read \"devices\"`.", "Press **Plan**. Each device shows what each port holds now and would hold. Nothing is sent to a device yet.", "Press **Apply** and confirm. The change is read back from each device. A device that refuses it, or does not hold it, is put back as it was."]],
      ["ul", ["It understands: *change the description for ports … to read \"text\"*, *clear the description on …*, *shut down …*, *no shutdown …*. Ports are written `gi1/0/1-24`, `te1/1/1`, `et1-48`, `po1`, or on Aruba CX `1/1/1-1/1/48`; the verb may come after the ports (*shut port 1/1/1 down*), several separated by commas. Anything else is refused with what it knows.", "Plain-English port changes are for Catalyst (IOS-XE), Arista (EOS) and Aruba CX; any other change, on any platform, is **Advanced change** below. A port a device lacks cancels the change on that device. A port that holds the address the gateway reaches the device on is never shut down.", "Only an account with full access sees this box. A read-only account, and an MCP key or address with `ro`, cannot change devices; one with `rw` can. Each change is written to the Log with who made it and what each port held before and after."]],
      ["h", "Advanced change"],
      ["ul", ["Sends the platform's own write. REST platforms (IOS-XE, Aruba CX, FortiOS, Infoblox, F5, Technitium): **Method** POST, PUT, PATCH or DELETE, the **Path** and a **Body**. PAN-OS and Panorama: **Method** is set, edit, delete, rename or move, **Path** the XPath, **Body** the XML. Arista EOS and NX-OS: **Lines** of configuration instead.", "**Plan** shows what the path holds now (Arista: the switch's own diff) and changes nothing. **Apply** asks, then sends. A REST PUT or PATCH that fails, or after which the device stops answering, is put back; Arista commits with a three-minute timer and confirms it when the switch answers; NX-OS rolls back to a checkpoint. A POST or a DELETE, and PAN-OS and Technitium changes, are not undone by the director.", "**Save** keeps the change over a reboot. Login, users, keys, file operations, reload and what would cut the management access are refused. Each change is written to the Log with who made it and the old state."]],
      ["h", "Capture packets"],
      ["ul", ["Captures for a few seconds on the devices the **Target** picks and shows a summary: the busiest address pairs, then the first packets one line each (headers only, never the data inside them). Works on the DNS Director, the NTP Director, Arista EOS and F5 BIG-IP; the other platforms say they cannot.", "On a director leave **Interface** empty for its gateway's interface. On EOS it is `Ethernet1` or `Management1` and the switch sees only traffic to or from its own CPU. On BIG-IP the default `0.0` is every VLAN.", "The **Filter** is tcpdump syntax made of words, numbers and `. : / ( ) _ -`, for example `host 10.0.0.5 and port 53`. At most 30 seconds, 2000 packets and 10 devices. The capture file is removed from the device afterwards.", "A capture shows other people's traffic, so only an account with full access sees this card, and an MCP key or address needs `rw`. Every capture is written to the Log with who made it and where."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Target", "Which devices: `all`, a name, `tag:TAG`, `platform:PLATFORM` or `gateway:N`, separated by commas."], ["Ask for", "Facts, Interfaces, Neighbors, Configuration or Show command."], ["Command", "The show command, for Show command only."], ["Change", "Change ports: the change in plain English; it acts on the devices the Target picks."], ["Method, Path, Body, Lines, Save", "Advanced change: see above."], ["Interface, Filter, Seconds", "Capture packets: see above."], ["Address", "Network tools: an IP address or host name to ping, trace (mtr), scan (nmap), look up or whois, from the node picked in the Node menu."]]],
      ["note", "Whatever a device answers is shown as text. It is data from the network, and an assistant is told to treat it the same way."],
      ["cli", "netdir --device-change \"change the description for ports gi1/0/1-24 to read \\\"devices\\\"\" --target sw1 [--dry-run] [--yes]\nnetdir --device-capture eth0 --target ns1 --capture-filter \"udp and port 53\" --capture-seconds 5\nnetdir --device-raw \"PATCH /restconf/data/…\" --device-body '{…}' --target sw1 [--device-save]   netdir --device-lines FILE --target eos1\nnetdir --ping ADDR   netdir --traceroute ADDR   netdir --scan ADDR   netdir --host-lookup NAME   netdir --whois NAME\nMCP: list_devices, device_facts, device_interfaces, device_ports, device_neighbors, device_neighbor_details, device_routes, device_routing, device_firewall, device_resources, device_vlans, device_api_catalog, device_api_get, device_show; device_change (rw only)\nAPI: GET /api/devices, POST /api/devices/change, GET /api/devices/query?target=all&op=facts\nEntra: netdir tshoot USER [HOURS] (or --troubleshoot-user USER [--troubleshoot-hours N]) (API: GET /api/devices/troubleshoot?user=…&hours=…; MCP device_troubleshoot_user)\nEntra browser sign-in: netdir --device-signin NAME (API: POST /api/devices/signin, then POST /api/devices/signin/poll)"],
    ],
  },
  config_devices: {
    title: "Devices",
    body: [
      "The switches and firewalls the gateway manages, and the logins it reaches them with. Both are shared by the cluster, so any node can answer for any device.",
      "Every change is saved and applied as you leave a row. There is no Apply button.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Add, change and remove devices.", "Add, change and remove the logins they use.", "Group devices with tags, so one request can ask a site or a role."]],
      ["h", "Add a device"],
      ["ol", ["Under **Logins**, right-click ▸ Add row: give the login a name, then a user name and password (Catalyst, Nexus, Arista, Aruba CX, Infoblox, Technitium, Observium) or an API token (FortiGate, Palo Alto, Technitium, Observium, IP Fabric).", "Under **Devices**, right-click ▸ Add row: its name, platform, management address, and the name of the login.", "Ask it for its facts on Monitor ▸ Devices."]],
      ["h", "What each platform needs"],
      ["ul", ["**iosxe** (Cisco Catalyst): RESTCONF. On the switch: `ip http secure-server`, `ip http authentication local`, `restconf`, and a privilege-15 user.", "**ios** (Cisco IOS, the classic software): SNMP, read only, UDP 161 (leave the port empty). Facts, interfaces, ports, LLDP and CDP neighbors, the routing table and any subtree by its OID; no running configuration (SNMP cannot hand it over). For v2c the login holds the community as its **token**; for v3 a user name, the authentication passphrase as the password and the privacy passphrase as the token. Options: `snmp_version` (2c or 3), `auth` (md5, sha, sha224, sha256, sha384, sha512), `priv` (aes, des, none).", "**sdwan** (Cisco Catalyst SD-WAN): the SD-WAN Manager, REST under `/dataservice`, a user name and a password. Its facts count the devices it manages; `show device` and the paths below `/dataservice` read the rest. Accounts, certificates and settings are not read.", "**nsxt** (VMware NSX-T): the NSX Manager (the cluster address), REST under `/api/v1` and `/policy/api/v1`, a user name and a password (HTTP basic authentication). Users, certificates and keys are not read.", "**aci** (Cisco ACI): the APIC, REST under `/api`, a user name and a password. Facts are the APIC's; interfaces and neighbors are those of every node of the fabric; the configuration is the tenants' configuration; `show <class>` reads any class of the object model. Logins, keys and certificates are not read.", "**nxos** (Cisco Nexus): NX-API. On the switch: `feature nxapi`, and a user with the network-operator role. Port 443 (`nxapi https port`).", "**eos** (Arista EOS): eAPI. On the switch: `management api http-commands`, `protocol https`, `no shutdown`, and a user with privilege 15 (the running configuration needs it). Port 443.", "**aoscx** (Aruba CX, 10.04 and later): the REST API with a user name and password; the driver logs in and out itself. On the switch: `https-server rest access-mode read-only` and `https-server vrf mgmt` (or the VRF you reach it on), and a user in a read-only group. Option `api_version` (default v10.09) names the REST version the release has.", "**fortios** (FortiGate): the REST API with a REST API admin's token (System ▸ Administrators ▸ Create New ▸ REST API Admin). A read-only profile is enough.", "**panos** (Palo Alto firewall) and **panorama**: the XML API, with an admin's API key as the token or a user name and password (a key is made from them). Give the admin an XML API role that allows Operational Requests and Configuration, read only. Options `vsys` (default vsys1) and, for a Panorama, `device_group`. A Panorama is also recognised under *panos*.", "**nios** (Infoblox NIOS): the WAPI with an admin user name and password; a read-only admin group is enough. Facts, addresses and health come from the grid members; networks, zones, records and leases are read with the API request. No neighbors, routes, VLANs, configuration or commands. Option `wapi_version` (default v2.12).", "**technitium** (Technitium DNS Server 15.0 and later): the HTTP API over HTTPS, port 53443 (turn on HTTPS for the web service; plain HTTP is never used). The login is an API token (Administration ▸ Sessions ▸ Create API Token) or a user name and password. Facts, listening addresses and statistics; zones, records, the cache and leases are read with the API request, which only sends get, list and query endpoints. No neighbors, routes, VLANs, configuration or commands.", "**meraki** (Cisco Meraki): the Dashboard API at `api.meraki.com` with an API key as the token (a read-only dashboard administrator's key reads only). Without options the device is the organization: facts, MX uplinks, and networks, devices, statuses, clients, SSIDs and the change log through Show and the API request. With the option `serial` it is one Meraki device: a switch's ports, its LLDP and CDP neighbors, and port changes in plain English (a switch's uplink is never shut down). Option `org` picks the organization when the key sees several.", "**entra** (Microsoft Entra ID): Microsoft Graph. It finds the devices of a user: **Show** takes `show devices user=<sign-in name, object ID or name>` and `show user user=…`; the API request reads `/users` and `/devices` only. Changes are `device_change_raw` (rw): method, a path at `/users`, `/devices`, `/groups` or `/organization` and a JSON body; the application needs the matching write permission; sign-in methods, passwords, keys, roles, mail and files are never touched. **Troubleshoot user** (right-click the device) looks a user up, takes the device they signed in with most recently and follows it on the Meraki network (a Meraki organization device in the inventory) for the last N hours: the client (IP, MAC, VLAN, SSID, the AP or switch and port, usage), its usage history and policy, for Wi-Fi the connection and latency statistics, connectivity events and failed connections, for a wired client the switch port's status and errors, and whether the AP or switch is online. The two are matched by name (the Entra device name against the host name the client gave), so a phone with another host name may not be found. Two ways to log in. **Browser sign-in** (nothing to register): add the device with a new login and leave user name, password and token empty, then right-click the device ▸ **Sign in with Microsoft**; the page shows a code and a link, you sign in there (multi-factor included), and the device then sees what that account may see in the directory (delegated User.Read.All and Device.Read.All; by default any member may read users and their devices, but a tenant can restrict it). Sign in again if the sign-in is revoked or unused for 90 days. **Application**: the login's user name is the application (client) ID and its password the client secret, with the application permissions `Device.Read.All` and `User.Read.All` consented by an administrator. Option `tenant` is the directory (tenant) ID or domain (browser sign-in: optional).", "**pbs** (Proxmox Backup Server) and **pdm** (Proxmox Datacenter Manager): the REST API on port 8007 (Backup Server) or 8443 (Datacenter Manager); certificate: usually *skip check*. The login is an API token: the token field holds `user@realm!id:secret` (a colon before the secret), or the user name holds `user@realm!id` and the token the secret; or a user name with its realm and a password (not for an account with two-factor login). Give the user and the token the Audit permission on `/` to read. Facts give the version, the uptime and the number of datastores or remotes; interfaces are the machine's network. **Show** takes `show NAME [store=NAME]` (Backup Server: `show datastore-usage`, `show snapshots store=NAME`, `show failed-tasks`, `show sync-jobs`, `show gc`) or `show NAME [remote=NAME]` (Datacenter Manager: `show remotes`, `show resources`, `show pve-qemu remote=NAME`, `show remote-tasks`); the API request reads any other path (not users, tokens, permissions, consoles or file downloads). Changes are `device_change_raw` (rw): method, path and a JSON body; on Backup Server a task is waited for. Never done here: users, tokens and permissions; certificates; consoles; restarting or shutting down the machine; wiping disks; deleting a datastore, backup group or snapshot (Backup Server); a prune that is not a dry run; removing a remote, deleting a guest or migrating one to another cluster (Datacenter Manager). Not run against a real server. **proxmox** (Proxmox VE): the REST API on port 8006 (certificate: usually *skip check*). The login is an API token (Datacenter ▸ Permissions ▸ API Tokens): the token field holds `user@realm!id=secret`, or the user name holds `user@realm!id` and the token the secret; or a user name with its realm (`root@pam`) and a password (not for accounts with two-factor login). A token with only the PVEAuditor role reads and nothing more. Facts name the cluster; interfaces are the node's network; **Show** takes `show NAME [node=N] [vmid=ID]` (`show vms`, `show storage`, `show nodes`, `show tasks`, `show backups`, `show ha`, `show node-status`, `show vm-config vmid=100`, `show ct-status vmid=200`; a guest's node is found from its vmid); the API request reads `/cluster/…`, `/nodes/…`, `/pools`, `/storage`. Changes are `device_change_raw` (rw): method, path below `/nodes`, `/cluster`, `/pools` or `/storage` and a JSON body; a change that starts a task waits for it and a failed task is an error with its log. Never done here: users, roles, permissions and tokens; cluster create, join and removal; shutting down a node (a reboot, `POST /nodes/NODE/status` with `{\"command\":\"reboot\"}`, is accepted); consoles and shells; certificates; deleting a VM, container or storage; restoring a backup or rolling back a snapshot; removing or wiping disks; commands and files through the guest agent. Option `node` is the node that node-scoped reads ask by default.", "**afc** (Aruba Fabric Composer, for Aruba CX VXLAN fabrics): the controller's REST API with a user name and password (the driver asks for a token and deletes it when done). Facts name the fabrics; **Show** takes `show <resource> [name=value …]` (`show fabrics`, `show switches`, `show vrfs`, `show vlans`); the API request reads `/<resource>` paths; changes are `device_change_raw` with a path below `/api/v1/` (users, authentication, licences, backups, upgrades and reboots are never touched).", "**bigip** (F5 BIG-IP, 13.1 and later): iControl REST over HTTPS (port 443), with a user name and password or an auth token. An account with the Guest or Auditor role reads. Facts, interfaces and port counters, routes and routing (static, BGP, OSPF, BFD), VLANs, AFM firewall policies, resources, and the API request (`/mgmt/tm/...`). No neighbors, configuration text or commands.", "**observium** (Observium monitoring, whose API is a feature of the Subscription Edition): the REST API over HTTPS (port 443; `$config['api']['enable'] = TRUE;` in config.php), with an API token (Profile ▸ API tokens; a read-only token reads only) as the token, or a user name and password. Without options the device is the Observium server: its facts say how many devices it monitors, resources are the alerts that have failed and the state of its devices, and **Show** takes `show device NAME`, which answers everything Observium knows about one monitored device in one go (its record, ports, addresses, neighbors, sensors, status, storage, memory pools, processors, inventory, counters, probes, alerts and its alert log; NAME is the host name, a short name that fits one device, or the Observium device ID), and `show NAME [device=NAME] [filter=value …]` for devices, ports, neighbours, sensors, status, storage, mempools, processors, inventory, alerts, alert_log, groups and the rest (`show alerts status=failed`; a list that is not about one device is cut to 500 rows, `pageno=2` asks for the next); the API request reads `/devices/…`, `/ports/…` and the other entities, and `/openapi.json`, Observium's own description of every answer. With the option `device` (a host name or Observium device ID) the device is one monitored device: its facts, ports with their counters, LLDP and CDP neighbors, CPU, memory, storage and sensors, and `show device` without a name. Option `prefix` is the path Observium is served below (`/observium`). Changes are `device_change_raw` (rw): method POST, PUT, PATCH or DELETE and a path below `/api/v0` (users, tokens and login are refused); Observium's documented API is read-only, so a build that does not accept the request answers with an error and nothing changes. SNMP communities and passwords are hidden. Neighbors, ports and sensors are read by the field names of Observium's database, and each answer keeps the whole row, so a field the director does not know is still shown.", "**ipfabric** (IP Fabric network assurance): the REST API over HTTPS (port 443). The login is an **API token** (Settings ▸ API tokens in IP Fabric; sent as `X-API-Token`) or a user name and password. The URL form is found by the driver (IP Fabric 7.5 and later use `/api/…` with no version; older ones `/api/v7.3/…`); option `api_version` forces one: `none` or `v7.3` and option `snapshot` (default `$last`) picks the snapshot. The device is the IP Fabric server: facts give the release and the number of devices in the snapshot, interfaces are the interfaces table (named `hostname:interface`), and **Show** takes `show devices|interfaces|sites|hosts|arp|snapshots [snapshot=ID] [columns=a,b] [limit=N] [start=N] [sort=col] [column=value ...]` (a value with `*` is a substring match), `show tables/<section>/<table>` for any other table, `show device NAME`, which answers the record, interfaces and ARP entries of one discovered device in one go, and `show diagnose`, which lists what IP Fabric answers (status and the start of the body) to a fixed set of requests, with and without the credential, to tell a wrong path from a refused credential. The API request reads `/tables/<section>/<table>?columns=...` (sent as a read), `/snapshots`, `/jobs`, `/version`. Changes are `device_change_raw` (rw): method POST, PUT, PATCH or DELETE and a path below `/api/<version>` (lock, load, unload or delete a snapshot, start discovery, add devices); users, tokens, login, single sign-on, licences and the system are refused. A column a release does not have is refused by IP Fabric: pick columns with `columns=` (the ? on a table in IP Fabric describes it).", "**aviatrix** (Aviatrix Controller): the controller's API at `/v2/api` over HTTPS (port 443; option `api_path` such as `/v1/api` for an old controller) with the controller's **user name and password** (the driver gets a session ID and renews it when it expires). The device is the controller: facts give the release and the number of gateways, interfaces are the gateways (state, addresses, size, cloud, region), and **Show** takes `show gateways`, `show transit`, `show spoke`, `show peerings`, `show gateway NAME` (the summary, details and transit peerings of one gateway in one go) and `show ACTION name=value …` for any read action (`list_…`, `get_…`, `show_…`; the API request reads the same as `/ACTION?name=value`). Actions about users, passwords, keys, certificates, tokens, single sign-on and backups are refused. Changes are `device_change_raw` (rw): method POST and a body `{\"action\": \"edit_gw_config\", \"gateway_name\": \"gw1\", …}` (or `action=…&name=value`); login, users and administrators, credentials, backups and restores, and deleting a gateway are refused. The action names are those of Aviatrix's Terraform provider.", "**centreon** (Centreon): the REST API v2 at `/centreon/api/latest` over HTTPS (port 443; option `prefix` if Centreon is served elsewhere, `none` for the root; option `api_version` for a fixed release such as `24.04`) with an **API token** (Centreon 23.10 and later) or a **user name and password** (the driver signs in and renews the session). The device is the Centreon central server: facts give the version and the number of hosts, interfaces are the monitored hosts (name, address, up or not), `show host NAME` gives everything about one host (monitoring record, configuration, services with status, timeline), `show hosts|services|problems|pollers|hostgroups|servicegroups|downtimes`. Changes (rw key) are POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE below `/monitoring` and `/configuration` (acknowledge, downtime, hosts, services, templates); *Save* exports the configuration to the pollers and reloads them. Users, contacts, access rights, tokens and administration are refused.", "**vcenter** (VMware vCenter Server): the vSphere Automation API at `/api` over HTTPS (port 443; vCenter 7.0 Update 2 and later, an older one is found by the driver, option `api_style` `api`, `rest` or `auto`) with a **Single Sign-On user name and password** (`user@vsphere.local`; a read-only role reads only). The device is the vCenter server: facts give the release and build, interfaces are the ESXi hosts, **Show** takes `show vms [state=] [search=]`, `show vm NAME|ID` (record, power, guest identity and network, VMware Tools in one go), `show hosts`, `clusters`, `datacenters`, `datastores`, `networks`, `folders`, `resourcepools`, `version` and `health`; the API request reads `/vcenter`, `/appliance`, `/cis`, `/content`, `/esx` and `/vstats`. Changes (rw key) are POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE below `/vcenter`, `/cis` and `/content`; a power action is `POST /vcenter/vm/ID/power?action=start`. Deleting a VM, host, cluster, datacenter, datastore, folder, resource pool, network or disk is refused; sessions, users, certificates, KMS, guest processes and files, console tickets, deployment, restore and the appliance are never changed.", "**nsone** (NS1 hosted DNS): the REST API at `api.nsone.net` (leave the address empty; option `prefix`, default `/v1`) with an **API key** as the token (Account Settings ▸ API Keys; a key with only read permissions reads only). The device is the NS1 account: facts give the number of zones, interfaces are the zones, **Show** takes `show zones`, `show zone NAME` (the zone and its records in one go), `show records zone=NAME [type=] [search=]`, `show record ZONE DOMAIN TYPE`, `show monitors`, `show datasources`, `show feeds source=ID`, `show networks`, `show qps` and `show usage`; the API request reads `/zones`, `/data`, `/monitoring`, `/networks`, `/stats`, `/views`, `/search` and `/account`. Changes (rw key) are PUT (create), POST (update; the old state is copied into the answer) and DELETE below `/zones`, `/data`, `/monitoring`, `/networks` and `/views`; deleting a whole zone is refused. API keys, users, teams, account settings, billing, TSIG keys and notification lists are never touched.", "**rcode0** (RcodeZero anycast DNS): the REST API at `my.rcodezero.at/api/v1` (leave the address empty; option `api_version` v1 or v2) with an **API token** as the token (Settings ▸ API Tokens). The device is the RcodeZero account: facts give the number of zones, interfaces are the zones, **Show** takes `show zones`, `show zone NAME` (zone, rrsets and query statistics), `show rrsets zone=NAME [type=] [search=]`, `show stats [zone=NAME] [kind=queries]`, `show messages`, `show secondaries` and `show settings`; the API request reads `/zones`, `/stats`, `/settings`, `/messages` and `/reports`. Changes (rw key) are POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE below `/zones` and `/settings`; records change with `PATCH /zones/NAME/rrsets` and a list of rrsets with `changetype` add, update or delete. Deleting a whole zone is refused; transfer keys, tokens and users are never touched.", "**junos** (Juniper Junos): the REST API over HTTPS (port 3443; on the device `set system services rest https port 3443 server-certificate NAME`) with a **user name and password**. Facts, interfaces and LLDP neighbors are read; *Show* takes any `show` command (not the configuration or secrets); *API request* reads `/rpc/get-NAME?argument=value`. Changes (rw key) are lines of configuration in set format (`set interfaces ge-0/0/1 description uplink`): loaded, checked, committed with a 3 minute timer and confirmed once the device answers, otherwise Junos undoes them; an operational RPC is POST `/rpc` with XML; a reboot is the line `request system reboot`.", "**dnsdir** (AnyName DNS Director) and **ntpdir** (NTP Director): the director's own MCP endpoint on its GUI port (53853 and 12380), with an **MCP key** as the token (on the director: Configure ▸ Monitoring ▸ MCP ▸ Keys; `ro` reads, `rw` also changes). Facts are the cluster node and release; interfaces and resources are the node's host statistics; neighbors are its LLDP neighbors; **Show** takes `show topology`, `show gateways`, `show alerts`, `show log`, `show query-stats`, `show host-stats`, `show nodes`, `show lldp` and, per product, `show dns` or `show ntp` and `show time` (optional `node=`, `range=`, `top=`); the API request reads any `/api/…` route; Configuration is the director's configuration with its secrets hidden. Option `node` makes the tools ask about one node of the director's cluster.", "**netdir** (MCP Network Director): another one of these, reached through its own MCP endpoint on its GUI port (22161) with an **MCP key** as the token (on that director: Configure ▸ Monitoring ▸ MCP ▸ Keys; `ro` is enough to read). Directors linked this way are a federation: each keeps the logins of its own devices, and the devices it reaches, and those below the directors it has as devices, are listed here like the others (Monitor ▸ Devices; *Via* names the directors between this one and the device). A request that picks them (a name, `tag:`, `platform:` or `all`) goes down to the director that has the login, which asks the device, hides its secrets and answers up the same way, so a client asks one director for a device in a DMZ. Links go from this director to the one below, so a firewall only has to allow that direction. A name another device here already has is shown as `name@director`. Reads go down for any target; a change or a capture goes down for a device named in the target (never for `all`, a tag or a platform), and the key of the link decides whether the director below allows it (a read-only key cannot change anything there). Facts, interfaces, resources and neighbors are the node's; **Show** takes `show devices`, `show nodes`, `show topology`, `show gateways`, `show alerts`, `show log`, `show host-stats`, `show schedule`, `show updates`, `show node-status` and `show lldp`; Configuration is that director's with its secrets hidden. Option `node` makes the tools ask about one node of its cluster."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Devices", "A table, one device to a row: **Name** (unique), **Platform**, **Address** (IP address or host name, no port), **Port** (empty for 443), **Login** (a login's name), **Tags** (separated by spaces, e.g. `site=dc1 role=core`) and **Certificate**: *skip check* for a device with a self-signed certificate, *check* to require one the system trusts. A CA of your own (`ca_pem`), the platform options (`vdom`) and `disabled` are kept from the file; set them with `edit_config` or in the file."], ["Logins", "A table, one login to a row: **Name**, **User name**, **Password** and **Token**. A row needs a password or a token. The secrets are never shown to an MCP caller or to a read-only account. Each password and token box has a small eye button to read what is saved.", ["Active checks", "How often a node connects to each device's API port while someone has Monitor ▸ Devices or Topology open or has asked a device over MCP in the last 10 minutes. 10 to 600; default 30. Shared."], ["Active facts", "How often a node asks each device for its facts (a login and a request) in that case. 1 to 60; default 5. Shared."], ["Idle checks", "The same connect when nobody is looking. 30 to 3600; default 180. Never quicker than the one above. Shared."], ["Idle facts", "The facts when nobody is looking. 5 to 1440; default 360. Never quicker than the one above. Shared."]]]],
      ["h", "How often the devices are looked at"],
      ["ul", ["Only the cluster's **primary** checks the devices (a node that is not in a cluster checks its own): a plain TCP connect to its API port (no login), and now and then a real request for its facts (which also proves the login still works).", "While nobody is looking (no Monitor ▸ Devices or Topology page open, no device request over MCP in the last 10 minutes) a node connects every **180 seconds** and asks for the facts every **360 minutes**. Someone looking brings it to every **30 seconds** and every **5 minutes**, and the first look checks at once. The facts are always asked when a device is first seen and when it answers again after being down.", "**Checks** (below Logins) changes all four intervals. The other nodes read the health from the primary over the cluster channel (a few seconds old), so 16 nodes do not mean 16 connections, and a look on any node speeds up the primary. If the primary cannot be reached, the devices show as not checked until the next primary takes over. A device that is added, enabled, disabled or edited is checked within 3 seconds. A device that goes down is noticed within one connect interval."]],
      ["note", "The logins are kept in the configuration file (mode 0600, root) and copied to the cluster's nodes over the pinned, signed cluster channel."],
      ["cli", "netdir --show-config"],
    ],
  },
  gateways: {
    title: "Monitor: Gateways",
    body: [
      "A table of the gateway protocol: for each gateway group and address family, which nodes take part and what each one is doing. It refreshes every 2 seconds. A star (★) marks this node.",
      "Every gateway is listed, whichever node the Node menu has picked. One that the picked node does not run is shown the way a node that serves it sees it (nothing is starred).",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Check which node is the **gateway controller** (it owns the shared address).", "See whether every node that should take part is heard, and whether its hellos are arriving on time.", "See whether each node is actually answering NTP on the address."]],
      ["h", "Reading the table"],
      ["ul", ["**Node name** is the node's host name, taken from the cluster. **Node IP** is the address the node uses in the gateway protocol. The name shows – for a node the cluster does not know, or until that node runs a version that reports its addresses.", "**AGC** (active gateway controller) is the one node that owns the shared address and answers ARP and neighbour requests for it. **AFN** nodes forward traffic and share the load. A node that is only listening, or that has not been heard from yet, has no role.", "**Pri** is the election priority (higher wins). **Slot** is the node's virtual-MAC slot: slot 1 always belongs to the controller, and other nodes take 2 and up. **Weight** is its share of client traffic.", "**State** is where the node is in the protocol. *active* is the controller. *forward* is a forwarder. *standby* is a node that has no slot yet (a node still in an election shows as standby too). *expired* is a node not heard from within the hold time. The node you are on shows its own exact state (listen, speak…). **preempt** marks nodes that take the role back as soon as they outrank the current controller.", "**Hello avg / max** is what the hellos really do, measured here: the average gap between a node's hellos, and the largest gap in the last 30 to 60 seconds. The largest gap includes a silence that is still going on, so a node that has gone quiet shows at once. The values are in milliseconds; compare them with the gateway's hello interval and hold time. A maximum close to the hold time means hellos are being lost or delayed and the node is close to being declared gone. It is empty until two hellos have arrived, and for this node itself.", "**Service** says whether the node's MCP and web listener is serving the gateway (“answering”) or not."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["If nothing is listed on a network that should have peers, check the group ID, the HMAC key and the interface on both nodes. Also check that multicast (or the unicast neighbour list in Configure ▸ Gateways) can pass between them."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --show-gateways\nnetdir --show-neighbors"],
    ],
  },
  nodes: {
    title: "Monitor: Cluster",
    body: [
      "A read-only list of every node that shares settings with this one. Adding, removing and promoting nodes is done on **Operate ▸ Cluster**; this page only watches.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      "Check that every node is reachable and running the same version, and see who the primary is. The list shows:",
      ["ul", ["Each node's **name** and its **IP** addresses (IPv4, IPv6 global and IPv6 unique local). Hover the name for the node's cluster address.", "Its **role**: primary or replica.", "Whether it is **reachable**, and its **epoch**.", "The version it is **running** and the version of the **source** it holds. They differ while an update is under way.", "When it was **last seen**.", "The node you are logged in to is marked with a star (★)."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --cluster-status"],
    ],
  },
  config_groups: {
    title: "Configure: Gateways",
    body: [
      "One card for every gateway. A gateway is a shared address (the *VIP*) that an assistant connects to; the nodes of the cluster agree between themselves who answers for it. This page holds the technical settings of that agreement.",
      ["note", "Gateways are **added and deleted on the Topology page**, not here. Use this page to tune one that already exists. If you are unsure about a value, leave it as it is: the defaults suit most networks."],
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Set which network card a gateway uses on this node.", "Set this node's priority and weight, and whether it takes the controller role back when it returns.", "Choose how clients are shared over the nodes.", "Tune the hello and hold timers.", "Change the key that signs the gateway messages.", "List the nodes by address when multicast cannot pass between them."]],
      ["h", "How the nodes agree on who answers"],
      ["ul", ["Every node of a gateway sends short *hello* messages. One node becomes the **gateway controller (AGC)** and owns the shared address. The other nodes, the **forwarders (AFN)**, share the traffic.", "The node with the highest **priority** is chosen as controller. If two are equal, the higher address wins.", "If a node goes quiet for longer than the **hold time**, the others treat it as gone and carry on without it."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: make one node the preferred controller"],
      ["ol", ["On that node, open **Configure ▸ Gateways** and find the gateway's card.", "Raise **Priority** above the other nodes' priorities (for example 200 against 100).", "Tick **Preemption**, so the node takes the role back whenever it is up. Leave it unticked if you would rather it waited until the current controller fails."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: change the key"],
      ["ol", ["Open the gateway's card and edit **HMAC shared key**.", "Save by leaving the field. The cluster pushes the new key to every node, and each node restarts the gateway.", "Expect the nodes to lose sight of each other for a moment. Do this at a quiet time."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: use a list of nodes instead of multicast"],
      ["ol", ["In a network that blocks multicast (a cloud network, for example), open **Neighbors (unicast mode)**.", "Add the address of **every** node of the gateway, this one included, one to a row.", "The nodes now talk to each other directly. The list is shared, so you only enter it once."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Name", "Optional label shown instead of the address. Shared."], ["Group ID", "1–255. Nodes with the same ID are one gateway; the ID also keys the election. Shared."], ["Interface", "The network card this node uses for the gateway (default `eth0`). This node only."], ["IPv4 VIP/prefix", "The shared IPv4 address and prefix, for example `10.0.0.1/24`. Empty turns IPv4 off for the gateway. Shared."], ["IPv6 VIP/prefix", "The shared IPv6 address and prefix. Empty turns IPv6 off. Shared."], ["More IPv4 addresses", "Further shared addresses in the VIP's subnet, one to a row of a table, without a prefix length. They fail over with the VIP. Shared."], ["More IPv6 addresses", "The same for IPv6. Shared."], ["Priority", "0–255, default 100. The election: the node with the highest priority becomes the active gateway controller (ties go to the higher address). This node only."], ["LB method", "How clients are spread over the forwarders: `roundrobin` (default; each new request goes to the next forwarder), `weighted` (in proportion to each node's weight), `hostpinned` (a client always gets the same forwarder) or `failover` (one forwarder takes everything, the rest stand by). Shared."], ["Weight", "0–255, default 100. This node's share of traffic under the `weighted` method; ignored by the others. This node only."], ["Hello interval (ms)", "How often a node announces itself, default 150. It cannot be larger than the hold time. Shared."], ["Hold time (ms)", "How long a neighbour may stay silent before it is considered gone, default 750. It cannot be smaller than the hello interval; keep it at least three times the hello interval, or one lost hello can look like a failure. Shared."], ["Max forwarders", "1–255, default 128. The most nodes that take part as forwarders (AFN). Shared."], ["HMAC shared key", "The secret that signs the gateway protocol messages. A new gateway starts with a random key. Every node of a gateway must use the same key, and a node with another key is ignored. Change it here and the cluster pushes it to every node, which restarts the gateway; for a moment the nodes do not hear each other, so do it at a quiet time. A key that is well known (`dnsdir`, `change-me`) or shorter than 12 characters is marked in red: anyone on the network who knows it can take the gateway over. Messages also carry the sender's clock and are dropped when it is more than 30 seconds from the receiver's or when they are not newer than the last one from that node, so a recorded message cannot be played back; keep the nodes' clocks in step (NTP). Shared."], ["Preemption", "Ticked: a node with a higher priority takes the active role back from the current controller as soon as it is up. Unticked: it waits until the controller fails. This node only."], ["Use real MAC addresses (no virtual MACs)", "On is the default for a gateway you add (gateways that already existed keep their setting). Off is the older way: each forwarder has a virtual MAC and the controller covers a dead node's MAC. On: no virtual MACs at all. Every node holds the VIP on its loopback, the controller answers ARP and IPv6 neighbor requests with the **real** MAC of the node it picks (it learns the others' MACs from their frames and asks for the ones it has not heard), and nothing is taken over when a node dies: the controller announces the VIP at its own MAC with an unsolicited ARP/neighbor advertisement, which only the neighbors that honor one will follow; the rest keep sending to the dead node until their ARP entry ages out. Needed where virtual MACs cannot work: a VMware port group with promiscuous mode off, a cloud that allows one MAC per interface. Shared by the cluster; changing it restarts the gateway on each node. The vMAC column of the Gateways page then shows real MAC addresses (the controller knows them all, a forwarder only its own)."], ["Neighbors (unicast mode)", "One IP address to a row of a table: list **every** node of the gateway, this one too (each node skips its own address). With none (the default) the nodes find each other by multicast; use the list when multicast cannot pass between them, such as in a cloud network. Shared: it replicates to every node."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Keep the hold time at least three times the hello interval. Otherwise one lost hello can look like a failure.", "A key that is well known or short (under 12 characters) is marked in red. Anyone on the network who knows it can take the gateway over.", "The Gateways page (Monitor ▸ Gateways) shows what the nodes are doing with these settings."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --show-config\nnetdir --show-gateways\nnetdir --show-neighbors"],
    ],
  },
  config_logs: {
    title: "Configure: Settings ▸ Logs",
    body: [
      "The **Logs** tab of **Configure ▸ Settings** says how much detail the log records and, if you want, sends a copy of the log to a syslog server so it can be kept and searched with the rest of your logs.",
      "**Every change saves and applies as soon as you leave the field or tick the box.** There is no Save button. If MCP Network Director refuses a value, a red *Not saved* message says why and nothing is changed until you correct it.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Choose how much detail the log records.", "Send the log to a syslog server over TCP, UDP or TLS.", "Check that the server is reachable, and send it a test message."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: change the log level"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Settings ▸ Logs**.", "Pick a value in **Log level**. Use `info` day to day and `debug` only while you are chasing a problem.", "That is all: the new level is used straight away. Read the log under **Monitor ▸ Log**."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: send the log to a syslog server"],
      ["ol", ["In the **Syslog** card, type the **Server**: a host name or an address, with the port if it is not the usual one.", "Pick the **Protocol**. **tls** is encrypted and the one to choose across a network you do not control; **tcp** does not lose messages while the connection is up; **udp** is the simplest and can lose messages silently.", "For **tls**, either give the **CA certificate file** that signed the server's certificate, or leave it empty to trust the system's authorities. Ticking **Do not check the server's certificate** turns the check off: the traffic is still encrypted, but anyone can pretend to be the server.", "Pick the **Facility** your collector expects (`daemon` is the default; `local0` to `local7` are for sorting a product's logs apart) and the **Message format**.", "Tick **Send the log to a syslog server**. The line under the settings shows *connected* once the server has accepted the connection.", "Press **Send test message** and look for it on the server."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Log level", "`debug`, `info` (default), `warning` or `error`. The least important lines the daemon writes to the journal and the Log page."], ["Send the log to a syslog server", "Switches forwarding on or off. Your settings stay when it is off. Forwarding is per node: tick it on every node that should send its log."], ["Protocol", "How messages travel. `tcp` (the default) keeps a connection and reconnects by itself; `udp` sends one datagram per message with no check that it arrived; `tls` is `tcp` inside an encrypted, authenticated connection (the usual port is 6514)."], ["Server", "The syslog server: a host name or an address, with `:port` after it if the port is not the usual one (514, or 6514 for `tls`). An IPv6 address with a port is written in square brackets: `[2001:db8::9]:514`."], ["Facility", "The syslog facility the messages are filed under. `daemon` is right for most; `local0` to `local7` let the collector sort this product's messages from everything else."], ["Message format", "`rfc5424` (the default) is the modern format with a precise time stamp. Choose `rfc3164` only for an old collector that cannot read it."], ["CA certificate file (tls)", "The PEM file, on this node, of the authority that signed the server's certificate. Empty trusts the system's authorities. Used only with `tls`."], ["Do not check the server's certificate (tls)", "Accept any certificate the server shows. The connection is still encrypted, but it is no longer proof that you reached your server. Use it to try things out, not for good. Used only with `tls`."]]],
      ["h", "What is sent"],
      ["ul", ["Every line the log shows, one syslog message per line, at the severity of the line: debug, info, warning and error become the matching syslog severities. The **Log level** above decides what is in the log, and so what is sent.", "The program name is `netdir` and the host name is this node's.", "On `tcp` and `tls`, an `rfc5424` message is sent with its length in front (RFC 6587 octet counting) and an `rfc3164` message ends with a new line. On `udp` each message is one datagram."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["A server that is down never holds MCP Network Director up. Messages wait in a queue; if it fills (2000 messages) the oldest are dropped and counted next to *sent* in the status line. The connection is retried every few seconds, slowing to every 30.", "The log is also still written to the journal and to `netdir.log` in the state directory, whatever happens to the syslog server. That file is rotated at 100 MB with the two previous files kept.", "The settings are for this node only and are not copied to the other nodes."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --syslog\nnetdir --syslog-server logs.example.com --syslog-protocol tls --syslog-ca /etc/ssl/log-ca.pem\nnetdir --syslog-facility local3 --syslog-format rfc5424\nnetdir --syslog-disable | --syslog-enable\nnetdir --syslog-test"],
    ],
  },
  config_general: {
    title: "Configure: Settings ▸ General",
    body: [
      "The **General** tab of **Configure ▸ Settings** has the **Neighbor discovery** card, which switches **LLDP** (Link Layer Discovery Protocol) on or off for the whole cluster. With it on, every node runs **lldpd**: the switches learn each node's name, port and management address, and each node learns which switch and port each of its interfaces is plugged into. **Monitor ▸ Node** then lists them under **LLDP neighbors**, which is the first thing to look at when a gateway's traffic goes to the wrong place after a failover. This is about the nodes themselves; the managed devices' own neighbor tables are on **Monitor ▸ Devices**.",
      "**The change saves and applies as soon as you tick or untick the box.** There is no Save button.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Switch LLDP on or off on every node at once.", "See whether lldpd is running on the node you are looking at (beside the box)."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: see which switch port a node is on"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Settings ▸ General** and tick **Run LLDP on every node**.", "Wait half a minute: a switch announces itself every 30 seconds or so.", "Open **Monitor ▸ Node**, pick the node in the **Node** menu, and read **LLDP neighbors**: the switch, its port, the VLAN and its management address, for each interface."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere.",
      ["fields", [["Run LLDP on every node", "Off by default. On: every node enables and runs lldpd; off: every node stops and disables it, also an lldpd started by hand or by its package. Each node checks once a minute. Beside the box, for the node you are looking at: **ACTIVE** while lldpd runs; **Stopped**; **lldpd is not installed on this node**; or, when AnyName (dnsdir) is installed on the same host, **managed in AnyName**, and the box cannot be changed here. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["**LLDP tells everything on the network segment the host's name, operating system and addresses.** That is usual in a data centre and is why it is off until you turn it on.", "lldpd is optional. The installer adds it where the distribution has it; on **RHEL, Rocky and Alma** it is in **EPEL**, which the installer does not switch on for you: install `epel-release` and then `lldpd` yourself.", "**This setting decides, whoever started lldpd.** Off, MCP Network Director stops and disables lldpd on every node within a minute, also one you started by hand or one its package started on install; to run lldpd on these nodes, switch LLDP on here. While LLDP is on, the file `/etc/lldpd.d/netdir.conf` (comments only) says so, and the uninstaller then stops lldpd; it never removes the package.", "**AnyName and MCP Network Director on one host share one lldpd, and AnyName decides.** While AnyName is installed, MCP Network Director neither starts nor stops it; switch LLDP in AnyName. MCP Network Director decides again within a minute of AnyName being removed.", "No neighbors? Many virtual switches and cloud networks do not pass LLDP. Some Intel network cards (i40e, ice) answer LLDP in their firmware and keep the frames from the operating system; turning the card's own LLDP agent off (`ethtool --set-priv-flags IFACE disable-fw-lldp on`) lets lldpd see them.", "lldpd also listens for CDP and the other vendor protocols only if you set that up in lldpd yourself, with a file of your own in `/etc/lldpd.d/`."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --lldp   (the setting, lldpd on this node, and the switch port of each interface)\nnetdir --lldp-enable | --lldp-disable"],
    ],
  },
  config_privacy: {
    title: "Configure: Privacy",
    body: [
      "Decides what an assistant that uses **MCP** is **not told**. The assistant is usually a public AI service: the answers a tool gives it (the public addresses of a firewall, the AS numbers of the upstream providers, serial numbers, e-mail addresses) travel to that service. Here you choose how much of that is replaced by short stand-ins before it leaves. It affects the MCP side only: the GUI shows everything its account may see.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Pick the **default level** for callers that have none of their own: **none**, **standard** or **strict**.", "Choose what **standard** and **strict** each hide: public and private addresses, AS numbers, e-mail addresses, SNMP contact and location, serial numbers, MAC addresses.", "Keep the first digits of an address readable (`203.0.` and a stand-in), so the network can still be recognised.", "List your own words (company, site and host names) that are always hidden."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: give one laptop a stricter view than the rest"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ MCP ▸ Keys** (or **Allowed sources**) and set the **Privacy** of its row to **strict**.", "Open **Configure ▸ Privacy** and tick what **strict** should hide, for example **Private IP addresses** and **MAC addresses**.", "Ask the assistant for a device's interfaces: the hidden values now show as stand-ins such as `PRIVATE-IP-3fa91c`."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Default level", "Used when neither the key nor the allowed source the caller came in by has a level. **standard** is what netdir always did (public addresses and AS numbers, e-mail addresses, contacts, serial numbers by their last four characters), so nothing changes until you choose otherwise. **none** hides none of it. Shared."],
        ["Public / Private IP addresses", "Public addresses are those of the Internet. Private ones are RFC 1918, carrier-grade NAT, link-local and unique-local ranges. Loopback, documentation addresses and netmasks are never hidden."],
        ["Public / Private AS numbers", "Private ones are the private-use ranges 64512-65534 and 4200000000-4294967294."],
        ["IP addresses", "Hidden (`x.x.x.x`, IPv6 `x:x:x:x:x:x:x:x`), or with the first 1 to 3 octets (IPv6: groups) shown (`203.0.x.x`), or the last 1 to 3 (`x.x.114.7`, `x:x:x:x:x:x:0:8888`). Each hidden part is an x, so two addresses that differ only in the hidden parts read alike. AS numbers and route distinguishers show their last three digits (`xx169`, `xx000:x00`)."],
        ["Serial numbers", "Shown, the last four characters (`****1234`), or hidden."],
        ["MAC addresses", "Shown, the vendor part only (`00:1a:2b:` and a stand-in), the network interface controller part only (`xx:xx:xx:3c:4d:5e`, the last three bytes, which identify the card), or hidden (`xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx`)."],
        ["Secret words", "One per line, 3 to 64 characters, any case: words of your own that mark a secret, on top of the built-in ones (password, secret, passkey, auth_pass, priv_pass, authentication_key, ciphertext, community strings, keys, tokens …). A key whose name holds one has its value hidden, a line of text that holds one is hidden from that word on, and a path or XPath that holds one is refused. One list for every filter and every level: secrets are hidden at **none** too."],
        ["Words always hidden", "One per line, 3 to 64 characters, any case. Used in every answer, including names of devices."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["**The strictest wins.** A caller that comes in by an address with one level and a key with another gets the stricter of the two.", "**Hiding is best effort.** Values written in unusual ways, an address split over two fields or a host name are not recognised.", "A stand-in the assistant sends back in a change (a configuration it read, edited and sent again) is turned back into the real value from the current configuration, as with passwords.", "A word that is also a device name hides the name too, and the assistant then cannot name that device in a request. Use the word list for names the assistant does not need.", "**Passwords, keys and tokens are always hidden**, at every level, including **none**."]],
    ],
  },
  config_upgrades: {
    title: "Configure: Settings ▸ Upgrades",
    body: [
      "The **Upgrades** tab of **Configure ▸ Settings** decides how MCP Network Director keeps itself up to date: whether a release you stage is installed everywhere by itself, and where **Fetch from online** downloads releases from. Staging and applying a release is done on **Operate ▸ Upgrade**.",
      "**Every change saves and applies as soon as you leave the field or tick the box.** There is no Save button.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Switch automatic updating on or off.", "Point updates at a different online repository (for example a fork)."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: turn automatic updates off"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Settings ▸ Upgrades**.", "Untick **Update every node automatically…**.", "Confirm the question. From now on a staged release waits until you press the update button on **Operate ▸ Upgrade**."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: use a different online repository"],
      ["ol", ["Type the address of the repository in **Online repository** (for example your own fork), and leave the field.", "Leave the field empty to go back to the default."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Update every node automatically when a newer release is staged (one node at a time)", "Auto-update. On (the default), each node builds and installs a staged release by itself, one node at a time (the first node must run it 2 minutes without trouble before the others follow), and rolls back if the new version does not stay up. Ticking it asks you to confirm. Applies to the whole cluster."], ["Online repository", "The GitHub repository **Fetch from online** (Operate ▸ Upgrade) downloads releases from, written as `https://github.com/owner/repo` (the default is `https://github.com/micush/netdir`; use it to follow a fork or if the repository moves). Saved when you leave the field; an empty field goes back to the default. Only github.com repositories are accepted. Shared: it is kept with the update settings on the primary and followed by every node. Nodes build and install what is downloaded as root, so point it only at a repository you trust. CLI: `--update-repo URL|default`."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Automatic updating is **on** by default, and it applies to the whole cluster. Nodes update one at a time, and the first one must run the new version for two minutes without trouble before the rest follow.", "Nodes build and install a downloaded release as root. Point the online repository only at a repository you trust."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --update-auto on|off\nnetdir --update-repo URL|default"],
    ],
  },
  config_web: {
    title: "Configure: Web GUI",
    body: [
      "This page has two parts: how people **sign in** to the console (the port it listens on, who may sign in, how long a session lasts), and the **certificate** the console is served with. Both are explained below, one after the other.",
      ["h", "Part 1: Sign-in settings"],
      "Every change saves as you leave the field.",
      ["h", "Sign-in: What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Change the port or address the console listens on.", "Choose which operating-system groups may sign in with full access, and which only to look.", "Set the idle timeout and the lockout rules.", "Set the shortest password the Users page accepts."]],
      ["h", "Sign-in: Step by step: change the port"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Web GUI ▸ General** and edit **Listen address**.", "Type just a port (for example `22161`) to listen on every address, or an address and port (`10.0.0.5:22161`) to listen on one.", "Confirm the question. The console restarts and **everyone is signed out**. Open the new address in your browser."]],
      ["h", "Sign-in: Step by step: give someone read-only access"],
      ["ol", ["Go to **Users ▸ Local** and add the person, choosing **Read-only** for their access. Or add them to the group named in **Read-only groups** yourself.", "They can now sign in and see every page, but every change is refused."]],
      ["h", "Sign-in: Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Listen address", "The port the GUI listens on, default `22161`, on every address; to listen on one address only, write it with the port, such as `10.0.0.5:22161` (saved as `listen` in the `web` block, where a bare port is kept as `:22161`). Changing it restarts the GUI and logs everyone out, so you are asked to confirm."], ["Login groups", "The operating-system groups whose members may sign in: one name or several separated by commas (default `netdir,netdir-users`). A member of any of them may sign in. The first one that is a local group is the one the Users page manages; `netdir-users` is meant for a group in Active Directory (or another directory, through sssd), which is kept there. A name that does not exist on a node is skipped. In a cluster this setting is **shared**: it is the same on every node, set on any of them. (A cluster whose login group is still just `netdir` shares nothing; the first change to anything else is shared.)"], ["Read-only groups", "Operating-system groups (default `netdir-ro`, the local group the installer creates; one name or several separated by commas, local or from a directory; empty turns it off) whose members may sign in **read-only**: they can open every page and see everything, but cannot change anything (every save, button and action is refused with a message, and Sign out still works). Someone who is also in a login group has full access. Keys and passwords in the configuration are shown as (hidden), and the saved configuration versions cannot be opened or downloaded. Add people on the Users page (choose Read-only when adding someone, or change an existing user's Access; the first local group of the setting is the one it manages), or with `usermod -aG netdir-ro alice`; a directory group is managed in the directory. The setting is shared by the cluster."], ["PAM service", "The `/etc/pam.d` service that checks passwords (default `netdir`). AD users through sssd: if the password is accepted but the log says `pam account check`, the daemon adds `ad_gpo_map_interactive = +netdir` to the domain section of `sssd.conf` itself at start (and restarts sssd); if it could not, add it yourself."], ["Session idle timeout (min)", "From 1, default 30. A browser is signed out after this many idle minutes."], ["Failed logins before lockout", "1–1000, default 3. Wrong attempts, within the window below, that lock out that address, and that address together with that user name. A user name alone is never locked out, so nobody can lock an administrator out by failing logins for that name. Shared by the cluster (this and the other lockout settings and the minimum length)."], ["…within this many minutes", "From 1, default 1. The window in which the failed logins are counted."], ["Lockout lasts (minutes)", "From 1, default 15. How long the lockout lasts."], ["Minimum password length", "1–128, default 8 (0 or empty is the default). The fewest characters a password set on the Users page, or with `--user-add` and `--user-password`, may have. Existing passwords are not checked. Changing a user's password, deleting the user or letting the account expire signs that user out of every open session. Shared by the cluster."]]],
      ["h", "Sign-in: The sign-in page"],
      "These two fields are on the sign-in page, not on the Configure page:",
      ["fields", [["Username", "On the sign-in page: an operating-system user who is a member of the login group."], ["Password", "On the sign-in page: that user's password, checked through PAM. After too many wrong attempts the sign-in page is locked for a while."]]],
      ["h", "Sign-in: Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["A **lockout** blocks the address, and that address together with that user name. A user name on its own is never locked out, so nobody can lock an administrator out by guessing at the name.", "Changing or deleting a user, or letting their account expire, signs them out of every open session.", "Single sign-on and directory sign-in are set up on the **Users** page (tabs SSO and LDAP)."]],
      ["h", "Part 2: The certificate"],
      "The certificate this console is served with. Until you install one, MCP Network Director makes its own *self-signed* certificate, which browsers warn about.",
      "The same certificate is used when you serve NTS to clients (Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Security).",
      ["h", "Certificate: What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Install a certificate you already have.", "Create a certificate request (CSR) for your certificate authority.", "Go back to the previous certificate, or make a fresh self-signed one."]],
      ["h", "Certificate: Step by step: install a certificate you already have"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Web GUI ▸ Certificates**.", "Paste the certificate into **Certificate** (your own first, then any intermediate ones), or choose the file.", "Paste the key into **Private key**, or choose its file.", "Press install. The pair is checked: the order of the chain, that the key fits, that it is still valid, that it can be used for a server, and its names.", "It is used from the next connection. No restart, and nobody is signed out."]],
      ["h", "Certificate: Step by step: get a certificate from your certificate authority"],
      ["ol", ["Fill in **Main name** (for example `gw.example.com`), and if needed **Other names** and **IP addresses**.", "Create the request. Copy the text from **Pending CSR** and send it to your certificate authority.", "When the certificate comes back, paste it into **Certificate**. Leave **Private key** empty: the key is already on this server.", "Install it."]],
      ["h", "Certificate: Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Certificate", "The certificate in PEM form, your own first and then any intermediate ones. Paste it or choose a file under it (**Certificate file**: `.pem`, `.crt`, `.cer` or `.txt`)."], ["Private key", "Its private key in PEM form, pasted or from a **Key file** (`.pem`, `.key` or `.txt`). Leave it empty when the certificate answers a request created here, because the key is already on this server."], ["Main name", "For a request: the common name, the main host name the certificate is for, for example `gw.example.com`."], ["Other names (one per line)", "For a request: further host names the certificate should cover."], ["IP addresses (one per line, optional)", "For a request: IP addresses the certificate should cover, so it can be used when you open the GUI by address."], ["Pending CSR", "The request that was created, read only. Copy it and send it to your certificate authority."]]],
      ["h", "Certificate: Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["**Revert** goes back to the previous certificate. **Regenerate** makes a new self-signed one.", "In a cluster, the certificate installed on the primary is copied to every member, and reverting it on the primary reverts them all.", "A certificate named in the configuration file overrides anything installed here.", "The page warns you when the certificate runs out within 30 days."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --show-config\nnetdir --configure       (interactive wizard)\nnetdir --config-import FILE\nnetdir --tls-status\nnetdir --tls-install --cert-file F --key-file F\nnetdir --tls-csr --cn NAME [--san NAME_OR_IP,...]\nnetdir --tls-revert\nnetdir --tls-regenerate"],
    ],
  },
  config_metrics: {
    title: "Configure: Metrics",
    body: [
      "Serves this node's counters at `/metrics` on the web GUI port, in the text format Prometheus reads. Each node serves its own numbers; Prometheus collects them from every node.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Choose which addresses and networks may read `/metrics`. There is no login: the address the request comes from is what counts."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: let Prometheus read the metrics"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Monitoring ▸ Metrics**.", "Under **Allowed sources** add the address of the Prometheus server (or its network).", "In `prometheus.yml` add a job whose targets are the nodes on the web GUI port, with `scheme: https`. If the web certificate is not one Prometheus trusts, give it the certificate authority, or set `insecure_skip_verify: true` for that job."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Allowed sources", "The addresses or networks that may read `/metrics` on the web GUI port (`10.1.1.50`, `10.1.0.0/24`, `2001:db8::/32`, or a host name such as `prom.example.com`, looked up again every minute). The page is always there, but while the list is empty nobody can read it, and anybody not listed gets 403."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["The metrics are numbers and states only: the version, how many devices are in the inventory, each gateway's state on this node and whether its listener is serving, when the certificates run out, and the host: CPU, load, memory, swap, disk I/O, each filesystem and each network interface. No device answers or credentials are in them.", "The source is the address of the connection. If Prometheus reaches MCP Network Director through a proxy or NAT, allow the address MCP Network Director sees.", "`/metrics` is on the same port and certificate as the web GUI and needs no new port."]],
    ],
  },
  config_mcp: {
    title: "Configure: MCP",
    body: [
      "Serves the Model Context Protocol at `/mcp`, on its own listener on every gateway address (the VIP, its further addresses and the anycast addresses) at port 443 unless you set another below; the GUI is not served there, only on the node's own addresses. So an assistant such as Claude can ask the managed devices (Configure ▸ Devices), look at the cluster and, from an address you allow, change anything the GUI can. Any node answers for the whole cluster; connect to the gateway's address so it survives a node going down.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Choose which addresses and networks may use `/mcp`, and which single addresses may also change things (**rw**). There is no login: the address the request comes from is what counts. An empty list is nobody.", "Add **keys** for callers whose address changes (a laptop on DHCP, Wi-Fi or a VPN): a key opens `/mcp` from any address, read only or read and change, sent as the header `Authorization: Bearer KEY`."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: use it from Claude Desktop"],
      ["ol", ["Add your computer's address under **Allowed sources** with access **rw** (or **ro** to look only). It is saved as you leave the row.", "Install Node.js on the computer. Claude Desktop starts local servers as programs, so `mcp-remote` relays to the URL.", "Add the server to Claude Desktop's `claude_desktop_config.json` (Settings ▸ Developer ▸ Edit Config), one entry per cluster, and restart Claude Desktop:\n`\"netdir\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [\"mcp-remote\", \"https://GATEWAY-ADDRESS/mcp\"], \"env\": { \"NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED\": \"0\" } }`", "`NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED` `0` accepts the self-signed certificate without checking it. To check it instead, leave that out and set `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS` to a file holding each node's `netdir-web.crt`, or install a real certificate.", "Ask, for example: *Which interfaces on the core switches are down?*, *What is plugged into port 1/0/48?*, *Back up the firewall's configuration*. Claude asks before each tool that changes something, unless you told it not to."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: use it from Claude Code"],
      ["ol", ["Allow the computer's address as above.", "Run `claude mcp add --transport http netdir https://GATEWAY-ADDRESS/mcp`. With the self-signed certificate, start Claude Code with `NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=0`."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: a key, for a computer whose address changes"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the **Keys** table and choose **Add key**, give it a name (who or what holds it, e.g. `laptop`) and the access, and press **Create key**.", "Click the eye in the new row's **Key** box and copy the key.", "Claude Code: `claude mcp add --transport http netdir https://GATEWAY-ADDRESS/mcp --header \"Authorization: Bearer KEY\"`. Claude Desktop with `mcp-remote`: add `\"--header\", \"Authorization: Bearer KEY\"` to its `args`.", "A lost or leaked key: right-click it ▸ **Delete** (whatever uses it is refused at once) and add another."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Largest answer (MB)", "The most one answer to an assistant may be, 1 to 8 MB (empty is 1). A longer answer is cut, with a note that says so. An assistant reads the answer into its own context, and 1 MB of text is roughly 250,000 tokens: more than many assistants take, so ask for less (a device, a shorter log range) before you raise this. Shared."], ["Allowed sources", "An address (`10.1.1.50`), a network (`10.1.0.0/24`) or a host name (`ops.example.com`, looked up again every minute), and the **Access**: **ro** may look, **rw** may also change things. **rw** is for a single address only: not a network, and not a host name, whose answer comes from DNS. Right-click a row ▸ **Disable** switches it off without deleting it (it is greyed out and allows nothing); **Enable** switches it on again. **Devices** says which devices a caller from the address may use: pills for device names, `tag:TAG` (every device with that tag, now and later) and gateways (the gateway and every device on it, now and later), **all** (no pills, the default) or **none**. Pick from the list or type a name and press Enter; × removes a pill. A caller that also has a key may use only what both allow. Shared."],
        ["Port", "The port of the MCP listeners on the gateway addresses, default 443; not the GUI's port. Shared; changing it re-binds them within seconds."], ["Keys", "Up to 32, each with a name and an **Access** (**ro** or **rw**), both changeable in the row (renaming does not change the key); right-click for **Add key**, **Copy key**, **Replace key**, **Disable** or **Enable** (a disabled key is kept but refused with 401 until enabled) and **Delete**. **Devices** limits the devices the key may use, as under Allowed sources: pills for names and `tag:TAG`, **all** (the default) or **none**. A limited key does not see the other devices (they are missing from list_devices and refused as if they did not exist); a target such as `all` or `tag:dc1` reaches the ones it may use; a change or capture that picks even one it may not use is refused whole. It is not offered get_config or the user troubleshooting tool, which reach every device and every key. A **gateway** pill (shown by the gateway\'s name) gives the gateway and every device on it, now and later; a caller with only device pills sees no gateway, and a limited caller sees only the gateways it was given in get_topology and get_gateways, and only the alerts about them and its devices. A request with a key's `Authorization: Bearer KEY` header gets that access from any address; from an address in the list above it gets the higher of the two. A key that matches none is refused (401), whatever the address, and logged once a minute per address. The key is kept in the configuration (so in its history and on every node) beside its SHA-256 hash, which is what a request is checked against; the **Key** box shows dots like the other secrets, and the eye in it shows the key. It is never shown to an MCP caller or a read-only account. A key made before v7 has only its hash and cannot be shown: right-click ▸ **Replace key**. The first gateway you add on Topology also makes one key, `default` (**ro**); change its access here. The address under the table is the first gateway's shared address (MCP port 443 unless set): connect to that, not to a node. Its changes are logged and kept in the history as `mcp:NAME@ADDRESS`. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "What it can do"],
      ["ul", ["**Devices (ro and rw):** **list_devices**, then **device_facts**, **device_interfaces**, **device_ports** (every counter and property of every port), **device_neighbors**, **device_neighbor_details** (everything LLDP and CDP report), **device_routes** (the routing table), **device_routing** (OSPF, BGP, EIGRP, IS-IS, RIP, BFD, static and policy routes), **device_firewall** (rules with hit counters, objects, NAT), **device_resources** (CPU, memory, storage, power supplies, fans, temperatures), **device_vlans**, **device_api_catalog** (what the device's own API offers) and **device_api_get** (any path of it, read-only: everything the named tools do not cover) and **device_show** with a target (a name, `tag:TAG`, `platform:PLATFORM` or `all`): every device picked is asked at once. **No MCP caller ever sees a public IP address, a public AS number or an e-mail address, and a serial number shows only its last four characters (`****1234`)**: an address becomes `x.x.x.x`, an AS number shows its last three digits (`xx169`) and an e-mail address becomes a token such as `MASKED-EMAIL-3fa91c` (private addresses and private AS numbers are shown), and a hidden value sent back in a change keeps the real value. **No MCP caller ever gets a device's keys or passwords**: the answers hide them, the running configuration is offered only by device_config with the secret words (Configure ▸ Privacy) hidden, and the parts of a device's API that hold users, keys, certificates and SNMP are refused.", "**Look (ro and rw):** any page's data (**api_get**, **get_config**), and the nodes, the gateways, the gateway election, alerts (one node or every node), the log, host load, the scheduler and upgrades, and the LLDP neighbors (which switch port each node's interface is on).", "**Change (rw only):** devices only (**device_change**, **device_change_raw**, **device_capture**). **netdir itself cannot be changed over MCP, whatever the access:** settings, the cluster, gateways, nodes, schedules, updates, alerts, users and certificates are changed in the GUI only, so a client cannot damage the gateway. Everything else here is read-only (**api_get** reads any route of the GUI's API; **list_api** lists them).", "**No address is shown a secret, rw included:** keys and passwords (gateway keys, device passwords and tokens, BGP/OSPF and LDAP passwords, SNMP communities, client secrets, join codes) read `(hidden)` in every answer, and the saved configuration versions cannot be opened or compared.", "Every request runs as the user `mcp@ADDRESS` (`mcp:NAME@ADDRESS` with a key) and is logged under that name; every route that is not a read, except the three device changes, is refused."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["**Privacy:** each key and each allowed source has a **Privacy** column (default, none, standard, strict) that sets what the caller is not told; the default and the rules are under **Configure ▸ Privacy**.", "The assistant reads text others control: device answers, host names, errors, log lines. Such text could try to tell it what to do, so keep the confirmation of changes on in your client.", "A web page cannot use it: a request from a browser page of another site is refused.", "Connectors added on claude.ai connect from Anthropic's servers, not from your computer, so they cannot reach it; use a local server in Claude Desktop, or Claude Code.", "The source is the address of the connection. Behind a proxy or NAT, allow the address MCP Network Director sees."]],
    ],
  },
  config_alerts: {
    title: "Configure: Alerts",
    body: [
      "Decides when MCP Network Director raises an alert, how long a problem has to last first, and where the alert is sent. The alerts themselves are on Monitor ▸ Alerts.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["The rules are grouped by what they watch, one row each: On, Severity, what opens the alert, its threshold, how long it must last (value rules only) and how long things must be good before it closes.", "Switch each rule on or off, and give it a severity (Info, Warning or Critical).", "Say how long a problem has to last before an alert opens: after some failed checks in a row, after some minutes, or when some of the last checks failed (for example 3 of the last 5). Rules about a value (CPU, memory, disk, certificate days) open when the value stays past the threshold for the minutes you give.", "Say how long things have to be good before the alert closes by itself: some good checks in a row, or some minutes.", "Send each opened and resolved alert to a webhook, and always to the log (the alerts appear there as lines of the Alerts type)."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: get alerts into a chat or ticket system"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Alerts**.", "Paste the address your chat or ticket system gave you into **Webhook address**.", "Press **Send test**. A test message should arrive there.", "Press **Save**."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: change a rule"],
      ["ol", ["Find the rule in the table and change its numbers. Only the rules you touch are saved, the others keep following the defaults.", "Press **Save**."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Webhook address", "Where a JSON message is posted when an alert opens and when it is resolved. The message carries `text` and `content` fields, so chat systems that take either show it, and the details (`node`, `severity`, `rule`, `title`, `subject`, `message`, `opened`, `count`, and for a resolved alert `closed`, `duration_seconds` and `resolution`). Empty means no webhook."]]],
      ["fields", [["Repeat every (hours)", "Tell the webhook again this often while an alert is still open and nobody has acknowledged or silenced it. 0 means tell once."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Alerts, acknowledgements and silences belong to the node that raised them. Monitor ▸ Alerts shows the node chosen in the top bar. The rules and the webhook are shared by the cluster.", "The engine looks at the node every 15 seconds; a \"check\" is one of those looks.", "An alert that closes and comes back within an hour is the same alert, with its count going up, so a flapping server does not fill the list.", "A resolved message is sent once, and not for an alert that is silenced.", "A rule that is switched off closes its open alerts.", "Closed alerts are kept for 30 days (at most 1000)."]],
    ],
  },
  config_backup: {
    title: "Configure: Backup",
    body: [
      "Every node copies its own state to one or more servers of yours over SFTP, and keeps only the newest few copies on each. With a backup you can rebuild a node from nothing, from this page or from the command line.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["See when this node last backed up and when it will next, and back it up now.", "Schedule the backups (a task on Operate ▸ Scheduler).", "List the servers the backups go to, test them, and put the nodes' SSH keys on them.", "Restore a backup onto the node picked in the Node menu."]],
      ["h", "The page"],
      ["ul", ["**Schedule**: when this node last backed up and when it will next, with **Back up now**. When backups run is a task of **Operate ▸ Scheduler** with the action **Back up this node**. **Schedule daily backups** adds one (every day at 02:30 UTC, each node at its own time within five minutes), and **Open Scheduler** goes there to change it (another time, hourly, weekly, only some nodes). \"Not scheduled\" in red means no such task runs on this node.", "**Servers**: one row per server. Under each row: when this node's last backup reached it, how many of this node's backups are there (hover for the file names) and, in red, why the last attempt failed. Click a row to pick it; the buttons above the table act on that server: **Test** logs in, **Install key** puts the nodes' keys on it, **Remove** removes it. **Add a server** adds a row.", "**Restore**: put a backup back onto this node.", "The settings save as you change them. The status and the restore are those of the node picked in the Node menu."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set up backups"],
      ["ol", ["On the backup server make a user that can write to a directory (for example `netdir-backup`). It needs a password only for the next steps.", "Open **Configure ▸ Backup**. In **Servers** fill in a row: the server, the user and the directory, and how many backups to keep. **Add a server** gives a row for another place; a backup goes to every row.", "Click the server's row, press **Install key**, type the account's password on that server and press **Install**. The password is used once, to put the keys of all the nodes of the cluster into the account's `authorized_keys` (restricted, so they can do nothing but file transfer), and is kept nowhere. Every node has its own key; `netdir --backup-status` prints it if you would rather add it to `~/.ssh/authorized_keys` yourself.", "With the row still picked, press **Test**. The first connection accepts the server's host key and remembers it.", "In **Schedule** press **Schedule daily backups**. To back up at another time, press **Open Scheduler** and edit the task **Back up every node**.", "Press **Back up now** and check that the file arrived."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "The servers are **Shared**: the same on every node.",
      ["fields", [["Name", "What the page calls the server (the host name when empty)."], ["Server", "The host name or address of a server that takes SFTP (a normal SSH server has it)."], ["Port", "Its SSH port. Empty is 22."], ["User", "The account the nodes log in as with their key."], ["Directory", "Where the files go. It is made if it is not there; empty is the user's home directory. All nodes use the same directory; each file name starts with the node's name."], ["Keep", "How many backups of each node stay on that server. After every upload the oldest beyond this number are removed. Empty is 14."]]],
      "One archive is made per run and sent to every server. A server that cannot be reached does not hold up the others; only that one is tried again (every 15 minutes), and the alert **Backup failed** names it. A server you add gets its first backup at once.",
      ["h", "What is in a backup"],
      ["ul", ["The configuration, its history, the web and DNS certificates with their private keys, the cluster identity and keys, the users, the alerts, the schedule, SNMP and the node's own SSH key for backups.", "Not in it: the log, statistics, downloaded block lists (they are fetched again), staged upgrades, and signed-in sessions.", "The files are not protected by a password: anyone who can read them can read the private keys in them. Give the backup servers' directories to as few people as you can."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: restore a node from this page"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Backup** with the node to restore picked in the Node menu. On a new node, install MCP Network Director and open its page; nothing needs to be set up.", "In **Restore** choose where the backup is. Your servers come first, and this node's backups on the one you pick are listed at once. **Another server** asks for its host, user, directory and the account's password, then **List backups**. **A file from this computer** takes a file you choose.", "To rebuild a lost node on a new one, tick **All nodes**: the list then shows every node's backups there, with their names.", "Pick the backup and press **Restore**. The backup is opened and checked, and a confirmation says whose it is and when it was made, with a note if it is from a newer version. For another node's backup the question is **Are you sure?**: this node then becomes that node (its identity, keys and certificates), so never do it while that node is still running.", "Confirm. The node swaps in the files and restarts, and the page comes back when it is up. The old files stay next to the state directory in a folder named `.before-restore-` and the time."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: restore a node from the command line"],
      ["ol", ["Install MCP Network Director on the machine as usual, but do not start it, or stop it: `systemctl stop netdir`.", "Copy the backup file from the server to the machine, for example `scp user@server:dir/NODE-20261025-023000.netbak /root/`.", "Run `netdir --restore /root/NODE-20261025-023000.netbak`.", "What was there is kept in a directory next to the state directory, named `.before-restore-` and the time. Delete it when you no longer need it.", "Start the service: `systemctl start netdir`."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["A failed backup is tried again after 15 minutes while a backup task runs on the node, and the alert **Backup failed** opens if it keeps failing (Configure ▸ Alerts). The Scheduler task's history says which servers took each backup and which failed.", "The files are written to the server under a temporary name and renamed when complete, so a cut connection leaves no half file among the backups.", "Backups made by versions 184 to 187 had a password. The page opens them with the password those versions had in the settings. On the command line `netdir --restore` asks for it (or give `--restore-password-file FILE`, or set `NETDIR_BACKUP_PASSWORD`).", "A backup file uploaded from your computer through the page can be up to 32 MB (5 MB for a node other than the one you are logged in to); use a server or the command line for a larger one.", "From the command line: `netdir --backup-status` and `netdir --backup-now`."]],
    ],
  },
  alerts: {
    title: "Alerts",
    body: [
      "The problems MCP Network Director has found on this node and not yet seen go away. An alert opens when a rule on Configure ▸ Alerts is met, and closes by itself once things have been good for the clear time you set there.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["See what is wrong, how serious it is, since when, and what has been done about it.", "Acknowledge an alert to say you are on it. This stops the repeat messages; it ends when the alert closes.", "Silence an alert for 15 minutes up to 7 days, so a known problem does not keep telling anyone. A silence covers that rule and that subject (for example that one filesystem) and stays until it runs out, even if the alert closes and comes back.", "Mark an alert resolved by hand.", "Run a safe fix, jump to the page that deals with the problem, or open the rule to change it."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: deal with an alert"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the alert.", "Choose **Acknowledge** to say you have seen it, or **Silence** and a time if it is expected.", "Choose **More** for the actions that fit this alert: a fix, when the rule has one, **Go to** the page that shows the cause, or **Edit rule**.", "If you fixed it yourself, choose **Mark resolved**. If the problem is still there, the alert opens again."]],
      ["h", "Reading the list"],
      ["ul", ["**Open**: the condition is bad and nobody has dealt with it. **Recovering**: the condition is good again, but the clear time has not passed. **Acknowledged**, **Silenced** and **Closed** are what they say; a closed alert shows whether it cleared by itself, was marked resolved, or its rule was switched off.", "**×3** means the alert has opened three times within an hour of closing, so it is flapping.", "Tick **Show closed** to see the last 30 days."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["The list shows the node chosen in the top bar. Alerts and silences are not shared between nodes.", "Prometheus sees only counts: `netdir_alerts_active` (open, not acknowledged, not silenced), `netdir_alerts_acknowledged` and `netdir_alerts_opened_total`."]],
    ],
  },
  config_snmp: {
    title: "Configure: SNMP",
    body: [
      "Lets a monitoring system read this node's status over SNMP. Access is **read-only**, over IPv4 and IPv6 on UDP port 161.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Turn SNMP on by adding a community string, and off by removing them all.", "Set the location and the contact the node reports.", "Choose the sources that may ask, with **Allowed sources**."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: turn SNMP on"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Monitoring ▸ SNMP** and add a row under **Community strings** (for example a long random word).", "Optionally fill in **Location** and **Contact**. Add the address or network of your monitoring system under **Allowed sources**: while that list is empty nobody can ask.", "MCP Network Director now writes `/etc/snmp/snmpd.conf` and starts `snmpd` itself. A green **ACTIVE** tag appears beside the heading once it is running on the node picked in the top bar.", "Open UDP 161 in the firewall for your monitoring system, and give it the community string."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: turn SNMP off"],
      ["ol", ["Remove every community string. `snmpd` is switched off. A configuration file that was there before is put back."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Location", "What snmpd reports as `sysLocation`, e.g. `Rack 4, Phoenix DC` (one line, up to 255 characters). Optional. Not shared: each node has its own, since it differs from node to node."], ["Contact", "What snmpd reports as `sysContact`, e.g. `noc@example.com` (one line, up to 255 characters). Optional. Shared."], ["Community strings", "One to a row of a table, up to 16; each gives read-only access over IPv4 and IPv6. SNMP is on while there is at least one and off when the list is empty. Shared. Each string is up to 64 letters, digits or punctuation (no spaces, quotes, backslash or `#`). They are kept in the config file and its history, readable by root only, and left out of support bundles."], ["Allowed sources", "The addresses or networks that may ask this node over SNMP (`10.1.1.50`, `10.1.0.0/24`, `2001:db8::/32`). A host name (`nms.example.com`) stands for the addresses it has when the configuration is written, and again every 5 minutes. While the list is empty nobody can ask. Others get no answer. Applies to every community string."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["An `snmpd.conf` that was there before is kept as `snmpd.conf.netdir-orig` and put back when the list is emptied. An snmpd that MCP Network Director never took over is left alone.", "The community strings are kept in the configuration file and its history, readable by root only, and left out of support bundles."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --show-config"],
    ],
  },
  config_cluster: {
    title: "Configure: Settings ▸ Cluster",
    body: [
      "How this node talks to the other nodes of its cluster: the port it listens on, the address the others use to find it, and how often it checks in. **Joining, promoting and removing nodes is done on Operate ▸ Cluster**, not here.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Change the cluster port or bind it to one address.", "Tell the other nodes which address to use for this one (useful behind NAT or with several addresses).", "Change how often replicas copy the settings."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set the address the others should use"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Settings ▸ Cluster** on the node.", "Fill in **Advertised address** as `host:port`, for example `10.0.0.5:22162`.", "Leave it empty to use this host's name with the listen port."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "These belong to this node, not to the cluster.",
      ["fields", [["Binding address", "The port the cluster listens on, which the other nodes connect to. Just the port (default `22162`) listens on all addresses; give an address with it, such as `10.0.0.5:22162`, to bind to one."], ["Advertised address", "`host:port` the other members use to reach this node. Empty means this host's name with the listen port."], ["Sync interval (s)", "1–3600, default 5. How often replicas pull state and members are polled."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["The cluster port (22162 by default) is separate from the console port (22161) and must be reachable between the nodes.", "Nodes pin each other's certificates and sign every request with the cluster secret.", "A shorter sync interval makes changes spread faster but asks the other nodes more often."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --cluster-status"],
    ],
  },
  anycast_bgp: {
    title: "Configure: Anycast ▸ BGP",
    body: [
      "Announce the gateways' **anycast addresses** to your routers with BGP. This page has two tabs: **BGP** (this one) and **OSPF** (next to it); use either one, or both. MCP Network Director drives FRR on this node: it writes `/etc/frr/frr.conf`, turns `bgpd` and `bfdd` on while BGP is set up, and reloads FRR. FRR must be installed on the node.",
      ["note", "If the AnyName DNS Director is installed on the same host, BGP and OSPF are **unavailable** here. AnyName manages FRR the same way and the two would overwrite each other's file, so MCP Network Director leaves FRR alone, ignores any BGP and OSPF settings in its config and refuses to set up or enable either. Anycast addresses still work as plain addresses on `lo`. Removing AnyName lifts this."],
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Set this node's AS number and router ID.", "Add the routers (BGP neighbors) this node talks to.", "Tune the timers and make a node the backup with *AS Prepend*."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: announce your anycast addresses"],
      ["ol", ["Add the anycast addresses to the gateway on the Topology page (right-click the circle ▸ **Add anycast address**).", "Open **Configure ▸ Anycast** on each node. Type this node's **Local AS number**. BGP now runs here.", "Press **+** above the neighbor table and fill in the router's **address** and **AS**. The neighbor is created once it has both.", "Set the matching password, if your router uses one.", "Watch **Monitor ▸ Anycast**: *Established* is a working session."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: make a node the backup"],
      ["ol", ["On that node, tick **AS Prepend**.", "It adds its AS three more times to every announcement. A longer path makes your routers prefer the nodes that do not have it ticked."]],
      ["h", "What it does"],
      ["ul", ["**Per node.** The AS, router ID and neighbors are not shared with the cluster — each node usually peers with its own upstream router. The anycast addresses themselves are shared (right-click a gateway on the Topology page → *Add anycast address*).", "**On when an AS is set.** BGP runs on this node while a local AS number is set. Clear the AS number to turn it off: the BGP section is removed from `frr.conf` and FRR keeps running. The **Router ID** can only be filled in while an AS number is set, and clearing the AS clears it too.", "**Announce only.** The node announces its anycast addresses and nothing else, and accepts no routes from its neighbors, so a peer cannot change this host's routing table.", "**Withdrawal.** An address exists in FRR's table only while MCP Network Director holds it on `lo` (its web listener serves, the gateway is not paused, MCP Network Director is running), so the route is withdrawn when the node cannot answer. **BFD** is on for every neighbor, sent at FRR's defaults (every 300 ms, 3 missed packets). The router drops a dead node after that, or longer if its own timers are slower; faster timers on the router cannot go below it. **Monitor ▸ Anycast** shows the time that results.", "An **IPv4** address is announced to IPv4 neighbors and an **IPv6** address to IPv6 neighbors; add a neighbor of each family to announce both.", "**Existing FRR setup.** The first time MCP Network Director takes `frr.conf` over, the old file is kept as `frr.conf.pre-netdir`. A change is applied with a reload, which needs FRR's `frr-reload.py` (on Debian and Ubuntu the `frr-pythontools` package, which the installer adds; on RPM distributions it is part of `frr`); without it MCP Network Director restarts FRR instead and the sessions drop briefly."]],
      ["h", "Neighbors"],
      ["ul", ["Press **+** above the table to add a row and fill it in: the neighbor is created once it has an address and an AS number. Click a row to highlight it and press **−** to remove it.", "Changes save when you leave a field. The password is an MD5 session password; it is kept in the config file and its history, readable by root only."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "These belong to this node, not the cluster. The anycast addresses themselves are shared.",
      ["fields", [["Local AS number", "This node's AS number, for example `64512`. BGP runs on this node while it is set; clear it to turn BGP off."], ["Router ID", "An IPv4 address that identifies this router. Optional; empty lets FRR pick one. Needs a local AS number: the field is disabled until one is set."], ["Keepalive (seconds)", "How often this node sends a BGP keepalive. Default `3`. Must be shorter than the hold time."], ["AS Prepend", "When ticked, this node adds its local AS three more times to the AS path of every anycast address it announces (`set as-path prepend ASN ASN ASN` in the outbound route-map). A longer path makes routers prefer the nodes that do not have it ticked, so use it on the nodes that should be the backup. Per node, like the rest of this card; it applies at once."], ["Hold time (seconds)", "How long a silent peer is kept before the session is declared down. Default `9`, minimum `3`. The session uses the lower hold time of the two ends, so a peer with longer timers agrees down to this."], ["AS of (a neighbor's row)", "The neighbor's AS number. A neighbor is created once it has an address and an AS."], ["Description of (a neighbor's row)", "A note of up to 60 characters shown with the neighbor."], ["Password for (a neighbor's row)", "Optional MD5 session password; it must match the router's. The eye beside the box shows or hides what is typed. Kept in the config file and its history, readable by root only."], ["Enabled (a neighbor's row)", "Off shuts this one session down (`neighbor … shutdown` in `frr.conf`) and nothing is announced to it; the neighbor stays configured. The same as right-click ▸ Disable under **Operate → Anycast**. Greyed out while BGP itself is off."], ["Multihop of (a neighbor's row)", "For an eBGP peer that is not on a directly connected subnet, for example an AWS VPC Route Server endpoint: the hop limit, usually `2`. Empty means directly connected. FRR then runs BFD to that peer in multihop mode as well, so the peer must be set up the same way (one side multihop and the other not leaves BFD down and the session resetting). Ignored for an iBGP neighbor."], ["New neighbor address", "The router's IPv4 or IPv6 address, for example `192.0.2.1` or `2001:db8::1`, in the empty row above the table."], ["New neighbor AS", "That router's AS number."], ["New neighbor multihop", "Optional, 2-255. See *Multihop* in a neighbor's row."], ["New neighbor description", "Optional note."], ["New neighbor password", "Optional MD5 session password."]]],
      ["cli", "netdir --bgp\nnetdir --asn 64512 --router-id 192.0.2.10   (--router-id needs an AS, set now or earlier)\nnetdir --keepalive 3 --hold 9\nnetdir --as-prepend on   (the local AS three more times on every announcement; off undoes it)\nnetdir --bgp-neighbor-add 192.0.2.1 --remote-as 64500 --description core\nnetdir --bgp-neighbor-add 10.0.1.5 --remote-as 64512 --multihop 2\nnetdir --bgp-neighbor-del 192.0.2.1\nnetdir --asn off   (also clears the router ID)\n(--password on the command line is visible to other users of the host; the page is safer)"],
    ],
  },
  anycast_ospf: {
    title: "Configure: Anycast ▸ OSPF",
    body: [
      "Announce the gateways' **anycast addresses** to your routers with OSPF instead of (or as well as) BGP. OSPFv2 carries the IPv4 addresses and OSPFv3 the IPv6 ones. MCP Network Director drives FRR on this node: it writes the OSPF part of `/etc/frr/frr.conf` and reloads FRR. FRR must be installed on the node.",
      ["note", "**ospfd** and **ospf6d** (FRR's OSPFv2 and OSPFv3 programs) are switched on in `/etc/frr/daemons` **only while OSPF is set up** on this node, together with `bfdd`. When you remove the last interface or press *Disable OSPF* (Operate ▸ Anycast) they are switched off again, so they do not run when nobody uses them. FRR is restarted when that happens."],
      ["note", "If the AnyName DNS Director is installed on the same host, BGP and OSPF are **unavailable** here. AnyName manages FRR the same way and the two would overwrite each other's file. Removing AnyName lifts this."],
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Choose the network interfaces this node speaks OSPF on, and the OSPF area of each.", "Set the router ID, the hello and dead intervals and the metric.", "Protect a link with an OSPFv2 key."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: announce your anycast addresses with OSPF"],
      ["ol", ["Add the anycast addresses to the gateway on the Topology page (right-click the circle ▸ **Add anycast address**).", "Open **Configure ▸ Anycast ▸ OSPF** on each node.", "Type the **Router ID**: an IPv4 address that is unique among your routers, for example an address of this node.", "Press **+** above the interface table, type the interface that faces your router (for example `eth0`) and leave the field. The interface is created and OSPF starts.", "Set the **Area** to the one your router uses (empty is area 0, the backbone) and the **Link type**. *Point-to-point* is the usual choice for a routed link and avoids electing a designated router.", "Watch **Monitor ▸ Anycast**: a neighbor in state *Full* is a working adjacency."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: make a node the backup"],
      ["ol", ["On that node, raise the **Metric** (for example to 100).", "Your routers then prefer the nodes with the lower metric and use this one when those are gone."]],
      ["h", "What it does"],
      ["ul", ["**Per node.** The OSPF settings are not shared with the cluster, like BGP's. The anycast addresses themselves are shared.", "**On when an interface is set.** OSPF runs on this node while at least one interface is listed. Remove them all to turn it off: the OSPF part leaves `frr.conf`, `ospfd` and `ospf6d` are switched off, and the Router ID goes with them.", "**Announce only.** The node announces its anycast addresses and nothing else (they are redistributed from `lo` through a route-map that permits only those addresses), and takes no routes from OSPF into this host's routing table, so a neighbor cannot change it. The networks of the OSPF interfaces themselves are part of OSPF and are announced as usual.", "**Withdrawal.** An address exists in FRR only while MCP Network Director holds it on `lo`, so the route is withdrawn when the node cannot answer. **BFD** is on for every interface; lower the timers on your router to shorten the time it takes to notice a dead node.", "**Both protocols.** You can run BGP and OSPF together. An address stays on this node while **either** carries it, and its pill on Topology shows the better of the two.", "**Existing FRR setup.** The first time MCP Network Director takes `frr.conf` over, the old file is kept as `frr.conf.pre-netdir`. A change is applied with a reload, which needs FRR's `frr-reload.py`; without it FRR is restarted for every change."]],
      ["h", "Interfaces"],
      ["ul", ["Press **+** above the table, type the interface name (the list offers this node's interfaces) and leave the field: the interface is created. The Router ID must be filled in first. Click a row to highlight it and press **−** to remove it.", "Changes save when you leave a field. The key is an OSPFv2 MD5 key (up to 16 characters); OSPFv3 does not use it. It is kept in the config file and its history, readable by root only.", "Untick **Enabled** to make an interface passive: no hellos are sent, no neighbors form, and the interface stays configured."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "These belong to this node, not the cluster. The anycast addresses themselves are shared.",
      ["fields", [["Router ID", "An IPv4 address that names this router in OSPF. It must be unique among your routers. It is needed as soon as there is an interface, and is saved together with the first one."], ["Hello interval", "How often, in seconds, hellos are sent on every interface. Empty or 10 uses FRR's default. It must be the same on the router at the other end, and shorter than the dead interval."], ["Dead interval", "How many seconds without a hello before a neighbor is declared down. Empty or 40 uses FRR's default. It must be the same on the router at the other end. BFD usually notices a dead neighbor much sooner."], ["Metric", "The cost the announced anycast addresses carry (empty or 20 is FRR's default). A node with a higher metric is the less preferred one, the OSPF way to make a node the backup. The addresses are announced as external routes of type 1, so the cost of the path to this node is added."], ["Area of (an interface's row)", "The OSPF area of the interface: a number (0 is the backbone) or a dotted address such as 0.0.0.1. Empty is area 0. It must be the same as on the router at the other end."], ["Link type of (an interface's row)", "*Default* leaves it to FRR (broadcast on Ethernet). *Point-to-point* is for a link between two routers and skips the designated-router election. *Broadcast* is for a shared segment. It must match the other end."], ["Key for (an interface's row)", "The OSPFv2 MD5 key of the link, up to 16 characters. Empty means no authentication. It applies to IPv4 only; OSPFv3 runs without it."], ["Enabled (an interface's row)", "Off makes the interface passive: no hellos, no neighbors, and the addresses are no longer announced over it. The interface stays configured. The same switch is under Operate ▸ Anycast."], ["New interface name", "The name of a network interface on this node, for example `eth0`. The list offers the ones that exist."], ["New interface area", "The area of the new interface; empty is area 0."], ["New interface link type", "The link type of the new interface."], ["New interface key", "An optional OSPFv2 MD5 key for the new interface."]]],
      ["cli", "netdir --ospf\nnetdir --ospf-router-id 192.0.2.10 --ospf-interface-add eth0   (the router id is saved with the first interface)\nnetdir --ospf-interface-add eth1 --ospf-area 0.0.0.1 --ospf-network point-to-point --ospf-key SECRET\nnetdir --ospf-hello 10 --ospf-dead 40 --ospf-metric 100   (- goes back to the default)\nnetdir --ospf-interface-del eth1   (removing the last one turns OSPF off and clears the router id)\n(--ospf-key on the command line is visible to other users of the host; the page is safer)"],
    ],
  },
  users_local: {
    title: "Users: Local",
    body: [
      "The accounts that may sign in to this web console with a user name and password. They are the members of the console's group (`netdir`, unless **Login groups** on Configure ▸ Web GUI says otherwise; the first of its groups that is local). They are ordinary operating-system accounts, so a password set here is the one PAM checks.",
      "In a cluster every change here is made on **every node**. The password hash is sent, not the password. If a node could not be reached the message says which, and the change can be repeated when it is back.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Add an account, or put an existing account on the machine into the console's group.", "Change a password or an expiry date.", "Disable an account (it keeps existing but cannot sign in here), or delete it.", "Give someone read-only access."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: add a person"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Users ▸ Local** and press **+ New**.", "Type the **Name** and a **Password**. Optionally set **Expires**. Choose the person's access: full or **Read-only**.", "Press **Add user**. The account has no shell and no home directory, so it can sign in here and do nothing else on the machine."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: change a password or an expiry date"],
      ["ol", ["Pick the account in the list on the left.", "Type a **New password** and/or change **Expires**. A blank date means the account never expires. From the date on the operating system refuses the account.", "Press **Save** (Enter in the password field does the same)."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: let an account that already exists sign in here"],
      ["ol", ["Press **Add an existing account**.", "Type its name in **Name**. The box suggests ordinary accounts that are not in the group yet.", "Nothing about the account is changed except that it joins the console's group. It keeps the password it already has. On the other nodes it is added to the group where it exists, and created with the same password where it does not."]],
      ["h", "Disable or delete"],
      ["ul", ["**Disable** takes the account out of the group and keeps the account. It works for any listed account except yourself and the last one. An account that is in the group only because it is its primary group is refused, with the reason.", "**Delete** removes the account. You cannot delete yourself or the last account that can sign in."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Name", "Optional label shown instead of the address. Shared."], ["Password", "On the sign-in page: that user's password, checked through PAM. After too many wrong attempts the sign-in page is locked for a while."], ["Expires", "Optional date after which the account cannot sign in. Empty means never."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Only accounts in the console's group are listed, and `root` never is. *Add user* never changes an account that already exists on a machine; use *Add an existing account* for that.", "A node that was down misses a change. Setting the password again sends the whole account again (and creates it where it is missing), which brings it back in line. `netdir --user-del NAME` can repeat a delete.", "A node that **joins** a cluster gets its existing accounts copied (with their passwords and expiry). An account that already exists on the new node is left as it is.", "Every member can manage every other member, which is the same power they already have over the daemon. Changes are logged with who made them.", "Changing a user's password, deleting the user or letting the account expire signs that user out of every open session."]],
      ["h", "Active Directory through sssd"],
      "Directory accounts (Active Directory, through sssd) can sign in without being created here. Do this on every node; the README has the full steps (*Signing in with Active Directory*).",
      ["ul", ["**Join the node**: `realm join -U Administrator example.com` (with `realmd`, `sssd`, `adcli` installed; the node needs DNS for the domain and a clock within 5 minutes of the controller). In the domain's section of `/etc/sssd/sssd.conf` set `use_fully_qualified_names = False`, `fallback_homedir = /nonexistent` and `default_shell = /sbin/nologin`, then `systemctl restart sssd`.", "**Make a group in the directory** called `netdir-users` and put the people in it. Its name must differ from the local `netdir` group: the local group is looked up first and would hide a directory group of the same name.", "**Login groups** (Configure ▸ Web) takes both, separated by a comma: `netdir,netdir-users`. That is the default for a new installation; an existing configuration is not changed by an update, so set it on each node. A member of either may sign in; a group that does not exist on a node is skipped. This page manages the first one that is local.", "**Group policy**: sssd refuses a PAM service it has no logon right for, which gives `pam account check (password accepted): Permission denied` in the log although ssh works. The daemon adds `ad_gpo_map_interactive = +netdir` to the domain's section of `sssd.conf` itself when it starts (the old file is kept as `sssd.conf.netdir.bak`) and restarts sssd.", "**In a container** (LXC, Proxmox) only the IDs of its `uid_map` exist (see `/proc/self/uid_map`), and sssd makes AD users' IDs from 200000 up, so the sign-in fails. The daemon then sets `ldap_idmap_range_min/max/size = 10000/60000/50000` in the domain's section of `sssd.conf` at start (unless the domain has a range of its own), clears sssd's cache and restarts it. Use the same range on every node.", "**Check** with `sss_cache -E`, `id NAME` (lists `netdir-users`), `getent group netdir-users` and `pamtester netdir NAME authenticate acct_mgmt`. Sign in with the name `id` prints. The reason for a refusal is in the log: `journalctl -u netdir | grep 'login failed'`.", "A directory account keeps its password and expiry in the directory, so those buttons refuse it here. *Add an existing account* can still add one by name to the local group instead of using a directory group."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --users\nnetdir --user-add NAME [--expires YYYY-MM-DD]\nnetdir --user-passwd NAME\nnetdir --user-expiry NAME --expires YYYY-MM-DD|never\nnetdir --user-del NAME\nnetdir --user-grant NAME\nnetdir --user-revoke NAME"],
    ],
  },
  users_sso0: {
    title: "Users: SSO ▸ General",
    body: [
      "Single sign-on lets people sign in with a Google or Microsoft (Entra ID) account, or with an account at any other OpenID Connect provider you run yourself (Authentik, Keycloak, Authelia, Zitadel), instead of a system password. **The password login stays, so you cannot lock yourself out.**",
      "A button for each provider you set up (**Sign in with Google**, **Sign in with Microsoft**, and a button with the name you chose for OpenID Connect) then appears on the sign-in page.",
      "This tab holds the table of **people who may use single sign-on** and the address of this console. The provider settings are on the **Google**, **Microsoft** and **OpenID Connect** tabs next to it.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Choose who may sign in with single sign-on, and with what access.", "Say which provider each person must use.", "Set the address the provider sends people back to."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: let someone sign in with single sign-on"],
      ["ol", ["Set up at least one provider on its own tab (Google, Microsoft or OpenID Connect). A provider is offered only when all of its fields are filled in.", "Back on **General**, add a row to **Users**: the person's **E-mail address**.", "Choose the **Access**: `rw` (may change settings) or `ro` (may only look). A new row starts as `ro`.", "Optionally choose the **Provider**: `any` (the default) or the one provider this address may use.", "Leave the field. The person can sign in now."]],
      ["h", "How it works"],
      ["ul", ["Only the e-mail addresses in the **Users** table can sign in, whatever account they use at the provider. Anybody else is turned away even with a valid Google or Microsoft account.", "The console checks the provider's signed ID token (signature, issuer, audience, expiry and a one-time nonce; PKCE is used). Google addresses must be verified by Google. Microsoft accounts must belong to the configured tenant.", "An SSO session is checked against the table every minute: removing an address ends it, and changing the level changes it.", "Addresses are not case sensitive.", "The settings are shared by the cluster. Each node needs HTTPS access to the provider (accounts.google.com, login.microsoftonline.com or your own provider)."]],
      ["h", "Which provider an address uses"],
      "Each row of the **Users** table has a **Provider** column. **any** (the default) lets the address sign in through any provider that is set up and vouches for it. **google**, **microsoft** or **oidc** admits it through that provider only, so an address that someone could also register at another provider cannot be used there. The log says which provider each sign-in used (`web: login: \"alice@example.com\" signed in with Google from ... (full access)`).",
      ["h", "The redirect address"],
      "Every provider has to be told where to send people back to after they sign in. That address is `https://THIS-NODE:22161/api/sso/callback`: the address in your browser's bar, then `/api/sso/callback`. In a cluster, register each node's address, or fill in **Address of this console** so every node uses one.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Address of this console", "Optional, `https://name:port` with no path. The provider sends the browser back to this address plus `/api/sso/callback`, so register exactly that with the provider. Empty uses the address the browser used to reach this node. Shared."], ["Users", "Who may sign in with single sign-on, as a table: the **E-mail address** and the **Access**, `rw` (may change settings) or `ro` (may only look); a new row starts as `ro`. **Provider** is `any` (the default) or the one provider the address may use: `google`, `microsoft` or `oidc`. Rows can be edited, dragged, filtered and right-clicked like the zone table. Addresses are not case sensitive. Up to 5000. Nobody else gets in through a provider, even with a valid account there. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["The **Users** table works like the other tables: rows can be edited, dragged, filtered and right-clicked (Edit, Copy, Paste, Add row, Move, Delete). Up to 5000 rows.", "The client secrets are stored in the configuration file like the other secrets and are not shown to read-only accounts.", "The log says which provider each sign-in used (`web: login: \"alice@example.com\" signed in with Google from ... (full access)`)."]],
      "The keys are in the `web` block: `sso_base_url`, `sso_google_client_id`, `sso_google_client_secret`, `sso_entra_tenant`, `sso_entra_client_id`, `sso_entra_client_secret`, `sso_oidc_name`, `sso_oidc_issuer`, `sso_oidc_client_id`, `sso_oidc_client_secret` and `sso_users` (one `alice@example.com rw` or `bob@example.com ro google` per entry; no level means `ro`, no provider means any). There is no separate command line for them; use `--config-import`.",
    ],
  },
  users_sso1: {
    title: "Users: SSO ▸ Google",
    body: [
      "Lets people sign in with a Google account. You register this console with Google once, then paste the two values Google gives you here.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Turn on **Sign in with Google** on the sign-in page.", "Keep the Google client ID and secret."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set up Google sign-in"],
      ["ol", ["In Google Cloud Console go to **APIs & Services ▸ Credentials ▸ Create credentials ▸ OAuth client ID**, and choose **Web application**.", "Add the redirect URI `https://THIS-NODE:22161/api/sso/callback`: the address in your browser's bar, then `/api/sso/callback`. In a cluster register each node's, or set **Address of this console** on the General tab.", "Copy the client ID and the client secret into the two fields below.", "Go to the **General** tab and add the e-mail addresses that may sign in. Only they get in."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Google client ID", "The OAuth client ID of the Google web application (ends in `.apps.googleusercontent.com`). Google is offered only when both the ID and the secret are filled in. Only addresses Google has verified are accepted. Shared."], ["Google client secret", "The OAuth client secret of the Google web application. Stored in the configuration file like the other secrets, and not shown to read-only accounts. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Google is offered on the sign-in page only when **both** fields are filled in.", "Only addresses Google has verified are accepted.", "The secret is kept in the configuration file and is not shown to read-only accounts."]],
    ],
  },
  users_sso2: {
    title: "Users: SSO ▸ Microsoft",
    body: [
      "Lets people sign in with a Microsoft (Entra ID) account of your organisation. You register this console in Entra once, then copy three values here.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Turn on **Sign in with Microsoft** on the sign-in page.", "Keep the tenant ID, the client ID and the client secret."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set up Microsoft sign-in"],
      ["ol", ["In Microsoft Entra go to **App registrations ▸ New registration**. Choose the platform **Web**, with the redirect URI `https://THIS-NODE:22161/api/sso/callback`.", "From the app's overview copy the **Directory (tenant) ID** and the **Application (client) ID**.", "Under **Certificates & secrets ▸ New client secret** make a secret and copy its **Value** (not its ID).", "Paste the three values into the fields below.", "Go to the **General** tab and add the e-mail addresses that may sign in."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Microsoft tenant ID", "The Directory (tenant) ID of your Microsoft Entra organisation, a GUID like `11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555`. Only accounts of this tenant can sign in (the token's issuer and tenant must match); `common` and domain names are not accepted. Microsoft is offered only when the tenant, client ID and secret are all filled in. The address is taken from the `email` claim, or from `preferred_username` when there is none. Shared."], ["Microsoft client ID", "The Application (client) ID of the app registration. Shared."], ["Microsoft client secret", "The client secret's **Value** (not its ID) from Certificates & secrets. Microsoft secrets expire: set a reminder to make a new one. Not shown to read-only accounts. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["**Microsoft secrets expire.** Set a reminder to make a new one before it does.", "Microsoft is offered on the sign-in page only when the tenant, client ID and secret are all filled in.", "Only accounts of this tenant can sign in (the token's issuer and tenant must match). `common` and domain names are not accepted.", "The address is taken from the `email` claim, or from `preferred_username` when there is none."]],
    ],
  },
  users_sso3: {
    title: "Users: SSO ▸ OpenID Connect",
    body: [
      "Lets people sign in with an account at your own identity provider, such as Authentik, Keycloak, Authelia or Zitadel. The provider appears on the sign-in page as a button with the name you give it.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Turn on a sign-in button for your own provider.", "Choose the button's name, and whether the provider's certificate is checked."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set up your own provider"],
      ["ol", ["In your provider, create an OpenID Connect client: a *confidential* client using the authorization code flow, with the redirect URI `https://THIS-NODE:22161/api/sso/callback`. The scopes `openid`, `email` and `profile` are used.", "Set the provider to sign ID tokens with **RS256** (an RSA key). Tokens signed another way, including the HMAC some providers use when no signing key is chosen, are refused.", "Mark the user's e-mail as **verified** in the provider. The address is taken from the `email` claim and the provider must say it is verified (`email_verified`).", "Fill in the issuer, the client ID and the client secret below, and give the button a name (for example *Company SSO*).", "Go to the **General** tab and add the e-mail addresses that may sign in."]],
      ["h", "Finding the issuer"],
      "The **issuer** is the address in the provider's discovery document, the part before `/.well-known/openid-configuration`. Examples: `https://auth.example.lan/application/o/netdir/` (Authentik), `https://auth.example.lan/realms/main` (Keycloak). It must be https, and the document must name that same issuer.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["OpenID Connect button name", "The text of the extra button on the login page, for your own OpenID Connect provider: *Sign in with* and this name. Up to 40 characters; empty is *SSO*. Shared."], ["OpenID Connect issuer", "The provider's issuer address, `https` and no path beyond the provider's own, for example `https://auth.example.lan/realms/main`. A trailing slash is ignored. The console reads `/.well-known/openid-configuration` below it. Shared."], ["OpenID Connect client ID", "The client ID of the confidential client registered with the provider. Shared."], ["OpenID Connect client secret", "The client's secret. Stored in the configuration file like the other secrets and not shown to read-only accounts. The provider is offered only when issuer, client ID and secret are filled in. Shared."], ["Do not check the provider's certificate", "For your own OpenID Connect provider only. On: the console accepts any certificate the provider presents, a self-signed one for instance, so the host does not have to trust your certificate authority. The connection is still encrypted, but nothing proves who is on the other end: somebody who can put a machine between this node and the provider could read the sign-in traffic and impersonate the provider, which would let them sign in as anyone on the Users list. Use it for a test or a network you trust; the log warns about it (at most once an hour). Off by default. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["The provider is offered only when the issuer, client ID and client secret are all filled in. An empty button name shows *SSO*.", "If the provider uses a certificate from your own certificate authority, that authority must be trusted by this host's system certificate store. Or tick *Do not check the provider's certificate* (less safe).", "That option keeps the connection encrypted but proves nothing about who is at the other end. Somebody who can put a machine between this node and the provider could read the sign-in traffic and impersonate the provider, which would let them sign in as anyone on the Users list. Use it for a test or a network you trust. The log warns about it (at most once an hour)."]],
    ],
  },
  users_ldap: {
    title: "Users: LDAP",
    body: [
      "Lets people sign in with an account from a directory, an Active Directory domain or a Samba 4 domain, using the ordinary user name and password form of the sign-in page. The host's own accounts are tried first; when they do not accept the name and password, the directory is asked. **The system login keeps working, so you cannot lock yourself out.**",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Point the console at your directory servers.", "Choose which directory groups get full access and which read-only.", "Try the settings with the **Test** box before anyone depends on them."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set it up (recommended, with a bind account)"],
      ["ol", ["In your directory make an account just for this, with no special rights. It only needs to read users and groups. With Samba: `samba-tool user create svc-console` (give it a long password, and `samba-tool user setexpiry svc-console --noexpiry` so it does not expire).", "Make the groups and add people: `samba-tool group add console-admins`, `samba-tool group add console-readers`, `samba-tool group addmembers console-admins alice`.", "Open **Users ▸ LDAP**. Add your server(s) to **LDAP servers**, for example `ldaps://dc1.example.lan:636`.", "Fill in **Search base** (the top of the domain, like `DC=example,DC=lan`), **Bind account** and **Bind password**, **Full-access group** and **Read-only group**. Groups can be a name or a full DN.", "Press **Save**, then use **Test** with a user name and password. It lists each step."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set it up without a bind account"],
      ["ol", ["Leave **Bind account** and **Bind password** empty.", "Fill in the servers, the search base and the groups as above.", "Each person now signs in to the directory as themselves. A name typed as `alice` is sent as `alice@example.lan`, the domain taken from the search base. `alice@example.lan` and `EXAMPLE\\alice` are sent as typed."]],
      ["note", "Without a bind account a session cannot be rechecked. Removing somebody from the group or disabling the account stops new sign-ins, but their open session runs until it expires or they sign out. Use a bind account when you need sessions to end at once."],
      ["h", "How it works"],
      ["ul", ["The console connects to a directory server over TLS (`ldaps://`, or `ldap://` with StartTLS: it never sends a password without encryption, and if StartTLS is refused it stops before sending anything). With a **bind account** it binds as that account, finds the person by `sAMAccountName` or by `userPrincipalName` (`alice`, `EXAMPLE\\alice` and `alice@example.lan` all work), checks that the account is enabled, and checks that it is a member of a group below. Only then is the password checked, by signing in to the directory as that person. (Without a bind account see the next point.)", "**Without a bind account** (leave the bind account and password empty) each person signs in to the directory as themselves, and the console reads their own groups and account status. A name typed as `alice` is sent to the directory as `alice@example.lan`, the domain taken from the search base (`DC=example,DC=lan`); `alice@example.lan` and `EXAMPLE\\alice` are sent as typed. This needs nothing set up in the directory, but a session cannot be rechecked: removing somebody from the group or disabling the account stops new sign-ins, and their open session runs until it expires or they sign out. Use the bind account when you need that.", "Membership counts through nested groups too (a group inside a group), the way Active Directory's own tools see it.", "Access comes from the groups: members of the **Full-access group** may change settings, members of the **Read-only group** may only look. Somebody in both has full access. An account in neither is refused.", "With a bind account, a session started this way is checked against the directory every minute, so disabling the account or taking it out of the group ends the session. If the directory cannot be reached for that check the session simply stays until it can be.", "Computer accounts are never signed in, a name that matches more than one account signs nobody in, and an empty password is never sent. The name typed in is used as a value in the search, never as part of its text, so it cannot change what is asked.", "Wrong passwords count toward the same lockout as every other login.", "The settings are shared by the cluster. The bind password is stored in the configuration file like the other secrets and is not shown to read-only accounts."]],
      ["h", "Samba and Active Directory"],
      ["ul", ["The **search base** is the top of the domain, like `DC=example,DC=lan` for `example.lan`. The **bind account** is its DN (`CN=svc-console,CN=Users,DC=example,DC=lan`) or its user principal name (`svc-console@example.lan`).", "Use port 636 (`ldaps://dc1.example.lan:636`). Samba makes itself a self-signed certificate (`tls.crt` under its `private/tls` folder): either make this host trust that certificate's authority, or tick *Do not check the server's certificate* (less safe, see below).", "List more than one server and they are tried in order; the first that answers is used."]],
      ["h", "The Test box"],
      "The Test box uses the settings as last saved, so press Save first after changing anything. A user name alone checks the lookup and the groups; a password as well also checks the password. It lists what happened step by step, such as which server answered, whether the bind account was accepted, which account was found, and whether it is in each group. A person's password typed here is sent to the console and used once for the check, and is not kept.",
      "With nothing typed, **Test** just connects and signs in the bind account. Without a bind account a person can only be tried with their password.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["LDAP servers", "The directory servers, one address per row: `ldaps://dc1.example.lan:636` (TLS from the start, port 636 unless written) or `ldap://dc1.example.lan:389` (StartTLS is required). Up to 8, tried in order. Shared."], ["Do not check the server's certificate", "On: any certificate the server presents is accepted, a self-signed one for instance, so the host does not have to trust your certificate authority. The connection is still encrypted, but nothing proves who is on the other end: somebody who can put a machine between this node and the directory could read every password typed here and answer as the directory. Use it for a test or a network you trust; the log warns about it (at most once an hour). Off by default. Shared."], ["Bind account", "Optional. The account the console looks people up with: a DN (`CN=svc-console,CN=Users,DC=example,DC=lan`) or a user principal name (`svc-console@example.lan`). It needs no rights beyond reading users and groups. Leave it, and the password, empty to have each person sign in as themselves (see above). Shared."], ["Bind password", "That account's password. Stored in the configuration file and not shown to read-only accounts. LDAP sign-in is on when servers, search base and at least one group are filled in, and the bind account and password are either both filled in or both empty. Shared."], ["Search base", "Where people and groups are searched, including everything below it: `DC=example,DC=lan`. Shared."], ["Full-access group", "A group (its name, like `console-admins`, or its full DN) whose members may change settings. Nested groups count. Shared."], ["Read-only group", "A group (name or DN) whose members may only look. Somebody in this group and the full-access group has full access. Shared."]]],
      "The keys are in the `web` block: `ldap_servers` (a list), `ldap_skip_verify`, `ldap_bind_dn`, `ldap_bind_password`, `ldap_base_dn`, `ldap_rw_group` and `ldap_ro_group`. There is no separate command line for them; use `--config-import`.",
    ],
  },
  history: {
    title: "History",
    body: [
      "Every change to the configuration is recorded: from the web console, the command line, a cluster sync or a file edit. Each version shows who made it and a plain summary of what changed. Secrets are hidden in the summaries. The newest 200 versions are kept.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["See what changed, when, and who changed it.", "Compare two versions.", "Go back to an older version.", "Save the configuration to a file, or load one from a file."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: see what a change did"],
      ["ol", ["Click a version in the list. It is highlighted, and the buttons in the list's header act on that one.", "Press **Diff vs previous** to compare it with the version before it, or **Diff vs live** to compare it with what is running now.", "Press **View** to see the whole version."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: compare any two versions"],
      ["ol", ["Tick the box on two versions (**Compare version**).", "Press **Compare ticked**."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: go back to an older version"],
      ["ol", ["Select the version and press **Restore**.", "It applies at once. The current configuration is saved as a new version first, so you can always come back."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: save or load a configuration file"],
      ["ol", ["**Download** saves the selected version as a file. **Snapshot now** records the current state as a version of its own.", "**Upload** asks for a configuration `.json` file (**Configuration file**), such as one saved with Download. It is checked first, and applied only if it is valid."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Compare version", "The tick box on each version. Tick two and choose **Compare ticked** to see what differs between them."], ["Configuration file", "What **Upload** asks for: a configuration `.json` file, such as one saved with **Download**. It is checked first and applied only if valid."]]],
      ["cli", "netdir --versions\nnetdir --version-show ID\nnetdir --version-diff A[..B]\nnetdir --version-snapshot [--note TEXT]   (a note can only be given here)\nnetdir --version-restore ID\nnetdir --version-export [ID]\nnetdir --config-import FILE"],
    ],
  },
  cluster: {
    title: "Operate: Cluster",
    body: [
      "A cluster keeps the settings of several MCP Network Director nodes in step, and lets you update them together. Every node starts as the primary of a cluster of one until it joins another. Clustering is separate from the gateway election, which works the same with or without it.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Add a node to the cluster, or take one out.", "Make a replica the primary when the old primary is gone.", "See whether every node is reachable and running the same version.", "Pull the latest settings to this node at once (**Sync now**)."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: add a node"],
      ["ol", ["On any member of the cluster, choose **Create join code**. The code works once and for an hour, and the member shows it read-only for you to copy.", "On the new node, open **Operate ▸ Cluster** and paste the code under **Join code**. Press **Join**.", "The new node's shared settings are replaced by the cluster's. Its interface, priority and weight stay its own.", "Add the node to the gateways it should serve (Topology ▸ right-click the gateway ▸ **Add node**). A node that has just joined starts out removed from all of them."]],
      ["note", "The new node does not offer the release it had staged to the others, so joining with a newer version never pulls the cluster up to it. A joining node on an **older** version still updates itself from the cluster."],
      ["h", "Step by step: make another node the primary"],
      ["ol", ["Pick the replica you want in the **Node** menu in the top right.", "Choose **Promote**."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: remove a node"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the member (not this node) and choose **Delete**.", "The member is dropped for good and nothing is kept about it. A copy that is still running resets itself to a cluster of one. Its address can be used by a new node.", "From the node itself, **Leave cluster** does the same."]],
      ["h", "Roles and membership"],
      ["ul", ["One node is the **primary**; the others are **replicas**. Shared settings are checked on the primary and copied out within the sync interval.", "If the primary is gone for good, choose **Promote** on the replica you want. Failover is automatic: a replica promotes itself when the primary has not answered for 5 minutes and more than half of the members, that replica included, agree; the one with the newest settings promotes and the others follow. It is on from three members and off with two. A replica cut off from the others never has a majority, so a network split cannot create two primaries; with two members only you can promote.", "Right-click a member (not this node) and choose **Delete**: the member is dropped for good, nothing is kept about it (a copy that is still running resets itself to a cluster of one), and it is not put on a removed list, so a new node can use its address. Command line: `--cluster-delete ADDR`. **Leave cluster** does it from the node itself. (`--cluster-remove` still drops a member but blocks its address and identity until `--cluster-unremove`, for a node that is still running and must not come back.)", "**Sync now** pulls the latest state immediately."]],
      ["h", "The epoch"],
      "The epoch is a counter that says which node is the primary. It starts at 1 and goes up by one each time a node becomes primary: when you **Promote** it, or when the primary is restarted, shut down (now, or a minute before a scheduled one) or updated on purpose and hands the role to the replica with the newest settings (it comes back as a replica, and the role stays where it landed). A primary that goes offline unexpectedly keeps the role until you promote another node or, with Failover on, the cluster does after 5 minutes; joins, syncs and members going offline never change it. Every member follows the primary with the highest epoch, so an old primary that comes back still claiming a lower epoch is ignored and falls in line as a replica. If two different nodes ever claim the same epoch (two admins promoting on either side of a network split), the nodes refuse the conflict instead of picking one. An epoch of 1 everywhere means nothing has been promoted yet.",
      ["h", "The Members table"],
      ["ul", ["**Reachable**: whether this node could ask that member for its status just now; when it says no, the reason is shown.", "**Epoch**: that member's view of the epoch (see above). They should all agree.", "**Running** and **Source**: the version the member is running, and the newest uploaded source version it holds.", "**Last seen**: when the member last answered."]],
      ["h", "Working on another member"],
      "Every page follows the **Node** menu in the top right, this one included. Pick a member, and Leave, Promote, Create join code and Sync act on it.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Node", "The menu at the top right of every page. It picks which member the page shows and acts on: this node (the default) or any other member of the cluster."], ["Join code", "On the new node: paste the code made by **Create join code** on a member. It is single-use, valid for an hour, and not shown again; the member shows it read only for you to copy."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Nodes pin each other's certificates and sign every request with the cluster secret.", "The cluster port (22162 by default) is separate from the console port and must be reachable between the nodes."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --cluster-status\nnetdir --cluster-token\nnetdir --cluster-join CODE\nnetdir --cluster-promote\nnetdir --cluster-delete ADDR / --cluster-remove ADDR / --cluster-unremove ADDR\nnetdir --cluster-leave\nnetdir --cluster-sync"],
    ],
  },
  updates: {
    title: "Operate: Upgrade",
    body: [
      "Update MCP Network Director in place. You give one node a release archive, and the other nodes copy it from there. Each node then builds and installs it, one at a time, and rolls back by itself if the new version does not stay up.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Upload a release archive (`netdir_vN.tgz` or `.zip`), or fetch the newest one from the internet.", "Start the update by hand, on this node or on chosen nodes.", "Watch each node's version, whether it is queued or updating, and the history of every update."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: update with automatic updating on (the default)"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Operate ▸ Upgrade** on any node.", "Choose the release archive, or tick **Fetch from online**, and press **Upload**.", "The release is checked and kept as the **source**. The other nodes pull it from there, and every node installs it by itself, one at a time.", "Watch the table: it says why a node is waiting. The first node is the canary; the rest wait until it has run the new version for 2 minutes with no bad gateway."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: update by hand"],
      ["ol", ["Turn off **Update every node automatically…** on **Configure ▸ Settings ▸ Upgrades**.", "Upload the release as above.", "Press **Update this node now**. Or tick one or more members (**Select**) and press **Update N selected**; those members update one at a time."]],
      ["h", "How updating works"],
      ["ul", ["**Update this node now** (top right of the Nodes card) installs the source here. In a cluster, tick one or more members first and the button becomes **Update N selected**: those members update one at a time.", "While auto-update is on, the button and the tick boxes are disabled; turn it off to update by hand.", "**Auto-update** (Configure ▸ General ▸ Upgrade) (on by default) makes every member update itself whenever a newer source exists. Withdrawing a queued update (`--update-cancel`) is command line only.", "Members update **one at a time**, and the first one is the canary: the others wait until it has run the new version for 2 minutes with no bad gateway, and wait while an update has failed on any member. The table says why, and the canary shows an hourglass next to its name (hover it for the seconds left). A node that stops answering while it installs an update shows as “restarting” (Topology: grey “updating”), not as down, and its gateway is grey, not red, until it is back. A node only takes itself down when another member has been serving every gateway it serves for at least 15 seconds, so the gateway is always answered by someone and clients have moved back after the previous node's restart; otherwise it waits and the table says why. “Update now” on a node that is not safe to take down asks you to confirm before forcing it.", "A new version that does not come up is rolled back automatically."]],
      "The table (refreshed every 2 seconds) shows each member's version, source, whether it is queued or updating, and failures. The history lists every update event (the newest 500 are kept), newest first, 25 to a page: use **‹ Newer** and **Older ›** to move through them. Use the **Node** menu in the top right to work on another member.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Release archive", "Select a `netdir_vN.tgz` (or `.zip`) release archive. It is checked and staged on this node; the other members pull it from here."], ["Fetch from online", "Tick it and **Upload** downloads the newest release of the online repository (Configure ▸ General ▸ Upgrade; `micush/netdir` unless you changed it) from GitHub (or, when it has no releases, its highest `v<number>` tag) to this node and stages it, instead of using the file you chose. This node needs to reach api.github.com and github.com (a proxy set in its environment is used). The rest is the same as an upload: the version must be newer than the one running before it can be applied."], ["Select (tick box on each member row)", "Ticks a member for **Update N selected**. Disabled while auto-update is on (Configure ▸ General)."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Each node builds the daemon itself, so it needs a Go toolchain, gcc and the PAM headers. The installer sets these up.", "A node that has just joined a cluster does not offer the release it had staged, so joining with a newer version never pulls the cluster up to it. Upload or fetch a release on that node to offer it. A joining node on an older version still updates itself from the cluster.", "The version in the archive must be newer than the one running before it can be applied.", "Withdrawing a queued update (`--update-cancel`) is command line only."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --update-status   (the 50 newest history events)\nnetdir --update-history  (all of them)\nnetdir --update-upload FILE\nnetdir --update-apply\nnetdir --update-push all|ADDR,...\nnetdir --update-cancel all|ADDR,...\nnetdir --update-auto on|off\nnetdir --update-repo URL|default"],
    ],
  },
  anycastop_bgp: {
    title: "Operate: Anycast ▸ BGP",
    body: [
      "Switch BGP on this node, or one of its neighbors, off and on without losing any setting. This page has two tabs: **BGP** (this one) and **OSPF**. The settings themselves are under **Configure ▸ Anycast ▸ BGP**; what BGP is doing is under **Monitor ▸ Anycast ▸ BGP**. The tab needs a local AS number to be set.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Stop announcing with BGP from this node (for maintenance) and start again.", "Shut down one session to one router and bring it back."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: stop announcing from this node"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Operate ▸ Anycast ▸ BGP**.", "Press **Disable BGP**. It stops BGP on this node as if the AS were cleared (the BGP section leaves `frr.conf`, the sessions go down, nothing is announced by BGP) but keeps the AS, router ID, timers and neighbors. The anycast addresses stay on `lo` while OSPF can still carry them; otherwise they are taken off and the gateways stop answering on them.", "Press **Enable BGP** to put it all back. The AS, router ID, timers and neighbors were kept."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: shut down one session"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the neighbor and choose **Disable**. The session is shut down, nothing is announced to it, and the neighbor stays configured.", "Choose **Enable** from the same menu to bring it back."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["A neighbor cannot be switched while BGP itself is disabled.", "It is per node, like the rest of the BGP settings. To switch another node, pick it in the **Node** menu at the top right.", "An address leaves this node only when **neither** BGP nor OSPF can carry it."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --bgp-disable\nnetdir --bgp-enable\nnetdir --bgp-neighbor-disable 192.0.2.1\nnetdir --bgp-neighbor-enable 192.0.2.1"],
    ],
  },
  anycastop_ospf: {
    title: "Operate: Anycast ▸ OSPF",
    body: [
      "Switch OSPF on this node, or one of its interfaces, off and on without losing any setting. The settings themselves are under **Configure ▸ Anycast ▸ OSPF**; what OSPF is doing is under **Monitor ▸ Anycast ▸ OSPF**. The tab needs at least one interface to be set up.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Stop announcing with OSPF from this node (for maintenance) and start again.", "Take one interface out of service and bring it back."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: stop OSPF on this node"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Operate ▸ Anycast ▸ OSPF**.", "Press **Disable OSPF**. The OSPF part leaves `frr.conf`, `ospfd` and `ospf6d` are switched off, the neighbors go down and nothing is announced by OSPF. The router ID, interfaces and timers are kept. BGP, if it is set up, carries on.", "Press **Enable OSPF** to put it all back; `ospfd` and `ospf6d` are switched on again."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: take one interface out of service"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the interface and choose **Disable**. It becomes passive: no hellos are sent, no neighbors form, and the interface stays configured.", "Choose **Enable** from the same menu to bring it back."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["An interface cannot be switched while OSPF itself is disabled.", "It is per node, like the rest of the OSPF settings. To switch another node, pick it in the **Node** menu at the top right.", "An address leaves this node only when **neither** BGP nor OSPF can carry it."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --ospf-disable   (--ospf-enable puts it back)\nnetdir --ospf-interface-disable eth0   (--ospf-interface-enable puts it back)"],
    ],
  },
  scheduler: {
    title: "Operate: Scheduler",
    body: [
      "Tasks that run by themselves, on every node of the cluster or on the nodes you choose: pause or resume a node, restart the host, update from online, back up the node (to the servers of Configure ▸ Backup), or run a shell command.",
      "The list of tasks is shared by the cluster, so the page looks the same through any node. What ran is kept per node, and shown for the node picked at the top right.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Add a task and say when it runs.", "Run a task now, on this node or on all nodes.", "Edit, copy, disable or delete a task.", "Read what ran, whether it worked, and the output of a command."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: add a task"],
      ["ol", ["Press **New task**.", "Give it a name and choose the **action**.", "Choose **when** (once, hourly, daily, weekly, monthly or yearly) and the time of day. Times are in UTC.", "Optionally set **To**, to give a window: each node then runs the task at a time of its own between the two, so they do not all act at once.", "Choose **Runs on**: every node, or the nodes you tick.", "Save. A task added or switched on starts from then; it does not run for times before."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: run a task now"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the task and choose **Run now on**.", "Pick **this node**, or **all nodes** (every node the task is for, started 1 minute apart, each when it is free)."]],
      ["h", "The page"],
      ["ul", ["**New task** opens the form; **right-click** a task for *Edit*, *Copy*, *Paste*, *Run now on* (*this node*, or *all nodes*: every node the task is for, started 1 minute apart and each when it is free), *Disable* / *Enable* and *Delete*. *Copy* remembers the task in this browser; *Paste* (in the menu, or on the button, or after right-clicking the empty list) adds a copy of it with a new name, which you can then edit. Double-click a task to edit it.", "**Next run (this node)** is when this node will run the task: the scheduled time plus this node's own place in the window (hover it for the scheduled time). The times are shown in UTC.", "**History of this node** lists what ran here, whether it worked, and the output of a command (click it to read it all). The last 300 runs are kept."]],
      ["h", "When a task runs"],
      ["ul", ["**Once**: one time, on the date you pick; **Hourly**: at a minute past the hour; **Daily**: at a time; **Weekly**: on the days you tick; **Monthly**: on a day of the month (the 31st means the last day in a shorter month, *last day* always the last), or on the *1st to 4th or last* occurrence of the weekdays you tick, such as the 3rd Saturday. When several are ticked (the 2nd and 3rd Saturday and Sunday) it runs *once* a month, on the first of those days, unless you choose *run on ▸ each of those days* (every matching day) or *one of those days, chosen per node* (each node takes one matching day of the month, the same one every time for that node and task, so the nodes do not all act on the same day; the time of day is then spread by To); **Yearly**: the same in one month. **Only in** (daily, weekly and monthly tasks) limits the task to the months you tick, such as April to October; none ticked means every month. *Every N* makes it every second, third… hour, day, week, month or year (counted from 1970 in the task's own zone, so it stays the same however often the node restarts).", "**Time of day** is UTC on every node, whatever zone the node or your browser is in. Next run, last run and the history are in UTC too; tick **Local time** at the top right of the history to see all of them in this browser's time zone (the choice is kept in this browser).", "**To** (optional) makes a window: the task is due at **At**, and each node runs it at a time of its own between **At** and **To** (At 01:00, To 10:00 means any time between 01:00 and 10:00 UTC; on an hourly task both are minutes past the hour, and the window is at most 59 minutes), chosen from the node's identity and the task, so the same node always gets the same place and the nodes do not all run it at once. Without **To** every node runs it at **At**. A node that was off and comes back inside the window still runs the task. A task saved by an older version with an offset window shows it as its **To** time. Tasks whose windows overlap are staggered on a node, so no two start less than a minute apart. Only one job runs at a time on a node: a job that falls due while another is running waits (the table says *waiting for another task*) and starts when that one has finished.", "**A node that was off or busy** runs a task it missed when it comes back, once, if that is still inside the window; older runs are skipped and the history says how many. A task that is still running when it is due again is skipped, and says so.", "**Runs on**: every node, or the nodes you tick. A task added or switched on starts from then; it does not run for times before."]],
      ["h", "Shell commands"],
      "A shell command runs with `/bin/sh -c` as root on each node, in the daemon's directory. The PATH has the usual system directories and no login profile is read. The output and the exit status are kept in the history, and the command is stopped when it runs past its time limit (the whole process group). Anyone who can sign in to this page can make a node run any command, so give the sign-in group (Operate ▸ Users) only to people you would give root.",
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["A shell command **fails** when it exits with anything but 0, or is stopped by its time limit. Tick **Ignore the exit status** for a command whose non-zero exit is an answer, such as `systemctl status` on a stopped service or `grep`: the run then counts as ok and its output ends with the exit status. A command that cannot start, or is stopped by the time limit, still fails. On the command line add `\"ignore_exit\":true` to `--schedule-set`.", "A **restart** or **reboot** waits until it is safe for the node to go down. **Update from online** installs the newest release only when it is newer than the one running.", "With auto-update on (Operate ▸ Upgrade) update from online is for one node of a cluster: the release staged there reaches the others, which update themselves. *Runs on* then offers one node only, and the server refuses the task for every node."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --schedule   (the tasks, and when this node runs them next)\nnetdir --schedule-history   (what ran here, with the output)\nnetdir --schedule-set '{\"name\":\"3rd Saturday\",\"action\":\"backup\",\"freq\":\"monthly\",\"nths\":[2,3],\"weekdays\":[6,0],\"at\":\"01:00\",\"until\":\"10:00\"}'   (add a task; give its \"id\" to replace one; actions: node_pause, node_resume, update_online, node_reboot, shell with \"command\")\nnetdir --schedule-del ID\nnetdir --schedule-run ID   (run it on this node now)"],
    ],
  },
  node: {
    title: "Operate: Node",
    body: [
      "What this node does, and the machine it runs on. The **Node** menu in the top right picks another member to act on.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Hand the gateway controller role to this node.", "Pause the whole node for maintenance, and resume it.", "Restart or shut down the whole machine, now or later."]],
      ["h", "Gateway controller"],
      "**Make this node the gateway controller** asks the current controller (AGC) to hand the shared address to this node. This node takes the role first and then the old controller steps down without taking its virtual MAC or its service down, so clients should lose nothing; a client that cached the old controller's MAC is still answered while the new one takes that MAC over. To prefer a node permanently, raise its priority (and turn on preemption) in Settings instead.",
      ["h", "Step by step: take a node out for maintenance"],
      ["ol", ["Pick the node in the **Node** menu.", "Press **Pause this node**. It resigns, stops answering and stops probing. The other nodes carry the traffic. Nothing is shut down.", "Do your work. **Resume this node** brings its gateways back once their servers answer."]],
      "**Pause this node** pauses every gateway on it at once (the same as pausing each gateway on the Topology page, but it also covers gateways added later). The node resigns and stops answering; the other nodes carry the traffic, the gateway controller role moves to another node, and if this node was the cluster's primary the primary role is handed to a running node too. Nothing is shut down, and the setting is kept across restarts of MCP Network Director and of the host. **Resume this node** brings its gateways back. It is useful for sending all traffic to one node, for example to benchmark it: pause the others instead of shutting them down. A gateway you paused yourself stays paused after the node is resumed.",
      ["h", "Step by step: restart or shut down the machine"],
      ["ol", ["Choose the **Action**: **Restart host** or **Shut down host**. It acts on the whole machine, not just netdir.", "Choose **When**: **Now**, **In** a number of minutes (up to 7 days) or **At** a time of day (24-hour, the host's clock).", "Press **Execute** and confirm."]],
      "A scheduled action is kept by the operating system, so it survives a restart of netdir. It shows here as a notice with **Cancel it**. A shut-down host stays off until it is powered on again, from its console or out-of-band.",
      ["h", "Protecting the gateway"],
      "An action started **now** is refused when this node is the only member serving one of its gateways, so a reboot never leaves clients without an answer; you are asked whether to go ahead anyway. The check is made when you press Execute, not at the scheduled time, so for a later time make sure another member will be serving by then. When a node stops, a controller announces that it is leaving, so another member takes over at once.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Action", "**Restart host** (the default) or **Shut down host**. It acts on the whole machine."], ["When", "**Now** (default), **In** a number of minutes, or **At** a time of day. Clicking into the minutes or time box selects its option."], ["Minutes", "With **In**: 1–10080 minutes (up to 7 days), default 5."], ["Time of day", "With **At**: a 24-hour time on the host's clock; if it has passed today it means tomorrow."]]],
      ["cli", "netdir --assert-agc\nnetdir --node-rename ntp02   (this node's host name and /etc/hosts; another node: right-click it on Topology)\nnetdir --node-pause\nnetdir --node-resume\nnetdir --node-status\nnetdir --power restart|shutdown\nnetdir --power restart --in 10\nnetdir --power shutdown --at 02:30\nnetdir --power status\nnetdir --power cancel\n(--yes skips the question and goes ahead even if a gateway would be left unserved)"],
    ],
  },
  host: {
    title: "Monitor: Node",
    body: [
      "How busy the machine MCP Network Director runs on is, on the node picked in the **Node** menu: CPU, memory, disk and network. MCP Network Director reads the numbers itself from the operating system every 10 seconds and keeps them for **30 days** (per-minute averages). They are saved every 5 minutes and when MCP Network Director stops, so a restart does not empty them.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["See at a glance how loaded the node is.", "Look back up to 30 days.", "Look at the whole cluster as one machine."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: look at a past period"],
      ["ol", ["Pick a range at the top (**Last Hour** to **Last Month**), or **Custom**.", "For **Custom**, fill in **From** and **To** (inside the last 30 days) and press **Apply**.", "Hover a chart for the value and the peak (the busiest 10-second sample in that point). A gap means the daemon was not running."]],
      ["h", "Reading the page"],
      ["ul", ["The **tiles** are the latest sample: CPU use (and the load average), memory in use (what is not available for programs, caches excluded), the fullest filesystem, how much of the time the busiest disk was busy, and network traffic in and out.", "The **charts** follow the range at the top (Last Hour to Last Month, or a custom start and end). Each point is an average; hover for the value and the peak (the busiest 10-second sample in that point). A gap means the daemon was not running.", "In a cluster, the **Node** menu's last entry, **Cluster**, shows the nodes as one machine: network rates, load averages, memory, swap and disk sizes and cores are **added**; CPU is the average over all the cores (a 12-core node counts three times a 4-core one), memory and each filesystem are per byte of the whole, disk busy is the mean, and a peak is the busiest node's (for the network, the sum of the nodes' peaks, an upper bound). The interface table lists every node's interfaces under its name. A line above the tiles appears only when a node could not answer, and names it.", "**Disk utilization** shows each filesystem's space in use (up to six, the largest first) together with how busy the disks were.", "**Network utilization** adds up the real interfaces. Loopback, netdir's own virtual-MAC links (`netdir1.1`), container and bridge plumbing (`veth*`, `docker*`, `br-*`, `virbr*`) and ports of a bridge or bond are left out so traffic is not counted twice. The interface table lists them all, except loopback (`lo`), with the link speed the driver reports and each link's use (the busier direction as a share of that speed; “–” when the speed is unknown, as on most virtual machines)."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["If memory use reaches 85% the **memory guard** drops the oldest history. The footer says so when it has.", "Memory use is the machine's, not netdir's. In a container or VM the numbers are what that system reports. Counters that go backwards (a reboot, an interface reset) give a gap instead of a spike."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Time range", "The buttons **Last hour**, **Last day** and **Last week** choose how much history the graphs show. They are per window; the history kept is 7 days."], ["From", "With **Custom**: the start of the range, a date and time inside the last 30 days."], ["To", "With **Custom**: the end of the range. Press **Apply** to load it."]]],
      ["h", "LLDP neighbors"],
      "With LLDP on (**Configure ▸ Settings ▸ General ▸ Neighbor discovery**), the card under the tables lists what each interface of the node is plugged into, as the switch announces it: **Switch** (its name; hover for its description), **Port** (hover for the port's ID when it also has a description), **VLAN** (*untagged* marks the port's own VLAN), **Management** (the switch's management address) and **Seen** (how long ago it was last heard; a switch repeats itself every 30 seconds or so). It is shown for one node, not for the cluster as one. *None seen yet* is normal for the first half minute, and for good on a network that does not pass LLDP (many virtual switches and cloud networks).",
      ["cli", "netdir --host\nnetdir --host --all-nodes   (the cluster as one machine)\nnetdir --host --host-range 1d\nnetdir --host --host-range 7d\nnetdir --host --host-range 30d\n(now, average and peak of CPU, memory, disk I/O and network, plus the filesystems and interfaces)\nnetdir --lldp   (the LLDP neighbors of this node)"],
    ],
  },
  anycaststatus_bgp: {
    title: "Monitor: Anycast ▸ BGP",
    body: [
      "What BGP is doing on this node right now. This page has two tabs: **BGP** (this one) and **OSPF**. It refreshes every few seconds. The settings are under **Configure ▸ Anycast ▸ BGP**; switching BGP or a neighbor off and on is under **Operate ▸ Anycast ▸ BGP**.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Check that a session to each router is established.", "Check which anycast addresses this node is announcing, and why one is not."]],
      ["h", "Reading the page"],
      ["ul", ["**Status**: the local AS, whether FRR is installed and `bgpd` answers, and what the last apply did.", "**Neighbors**: the BGP session state read from FRR. *Established* is a working session. A neighbor you disabled shows *disabled*. Also shown: the **BFD** state (*up* means the router answers fast failure detection, *down* means it does not) and, beside it, how long the router takes to drop this node once it goes silent, worked out from the timers FRR agreed with it (hover for the numbers; the time this node takes to drop the router is in the hover too), how long the session has been up, and how many prefixes were sent to it.", "**Anycast addresses**: every anycast address of every gateway and whether this node announces it. An address is *withdrawn* while this node's listener is not serving, the gateway is paused or not running here, or the address could not be added to `lo`. The reason is shown."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: find out why an address is not announced"],
      ["ol", ["Find the address in the **Anycast addresses** list. If it is *withdrawn*, read the reason beside it.", "If the reason is the listener or the gateway, look at **Topology**.", "If the reason is BGP, look at **Neighbors** for a session that is not *Established*. If you also use OSPF, check its tab: an address is announced while either protocol carries it."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --bgp\nnetdir --bgp-neighbor-add 192.0.2.1 --remote-as 64500 --description core\nnetdir --bgp-neighbor-add 10.0.1.5 --remote-as 64512 --multihop 2\nnetdir --bgp-neighbor-del 192.0.2.1\nnetdir --bgp-disable\nnetdir --bgp-enable\nnetdir --bgp-neighbor-disable 192.0.2.1\nnetdir --bgp-neighbor-enable 192.0.2.1"],
    ],
  },
  anycaststatus_ospf: {
    title: "Monitor: Anycast ▸ OSPF",
    body: [
      "What OSPF is doing on this node right now. This page has two tabs: **BGP** and **OSPF** (this one). It refreshes every few seconds. The settings are under **Configure ▸ Anycast ▸ OSPF**; switching OSPF or an interface off and on is under **Operate ▸ Anycast ▸ OSPF**.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Check that every interface has a neighbor in state Full.", "Check which anycast addresses this node is announcing, and why one is not."]],
      ["h", "Reading the page"],
      ["ul", ["**Status**: the router ID, whether FRR is installed and `ospfd` and `ospf6d` answer, and what the last apply did.", "**Neighbors**: every interface with its area and, for each neighbor on it, the neighbor's router ID, the protocol (OSPFv2 for IPv4, OSPFv3 for IPv6) and its state. *Full* is a working adjacency; *2-Way*, *ExStart*, *Exchange* and *Loading* are on the way there; an interface with no neighbor shows *no neighbor*, and one you disabled shows *disabled*. Also shown: the **BFD** state (read for OSPFv2 neighbors) and how long the neighbor has been up.", "**Anycast addresses**: every anycast address of every gateway and whether this node announces it. An address is *withdrawn* while this node's listener is not serving, the gateway is paused or not running here, or the address could not be added to `lo`. The reason is shown."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: find out why an address is not announced"],
      ["ol", ["Find the address in the **Anycast addresses** list. If it is *withdrawn*, read the reason beside it.", "If the reason is the listener or the gateway, look at **Topology**.", "If the reason is OSPF, look at **Neighbors** for an interface whose neighbor is not *Full*, and check the area, link type, key and timers against the router at the other end. If you also use BGP, check its tab: an address is announced while either protocol carries it."]],
      ["cli", "netdir --ospf"],
    ],
  },
  capture: {
    title: "Monitor: Capture",
    body: [
      "A packet capture, like `tcpdump`, on one node or on every node of the cluster at once. It only listens: nothing is sent and nothing is changed. It is off until you press Start.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Watch live what a node sends and receives.", "Save the capture as a `.pcap` file for Wireshark.", "Capture on all nodes at the same moment, to see whether what one node sent is what another received."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: capture on one node"],
      ["ol", ["Pick the node in the **Node** menu.", "Choose the **Interface**. A gateway's interface is marked; the virtual-MAC interfaces are there too.", "Optionally type a **Filter** (see below) and press **Start**.", "The newest packets are listed live. Press **Stop** to end it. What was captured stays until you start again.", "**Download .tgz** saves it as an archive with one `.pcap` in it. **Clear** empties the list."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: capture on every node"],
      ["ol", ["Choose **Cluster** in the Node menu.", "Choose the **Interface** (every node must have one of that name) and **How long** (5, 10, 30 or 60 seconds).", "Press **Capture on all nodes**, then **Download .tgz**. You get one `.pcap` per node (this node's is marked `-this-node`), a `summary.txt`, and an `errors.txt` naming any node that could not capture and why."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: capture one gateway"],
      ["ol", ["Choose the **Gateway** in the Node menu.", "Optionally type a **Filter** and choose **How long**. The interface is the gateway's (shown in place of the Interface menu).", "Press **Capture on its nodes**, then **Download .tgz**. It runs only on the nodes that serve the gateway and keeps only the packets to and from its addresses (VIPs, further shared addresses and anycast addresses); your filter narrows that further."]],
      ["h", "One node"],
      ["ul", ["The capture runs on the node chosen in the **Node** menu. Pick an **interface** (a gateway's interface is marked, and the virtual-MAC interfaces `netdirN.M` are there too), optionally a **filter**, and press **Start**.", "The newest packets are listed live, one line each: TCP flags, ARP and IPv6 neighbor discovery with the Ethernet addresses they were sent from and to (`ARP, 10.20.0.205 is-at 00:1a:7c:01:02:00 (eth 02:… > …)`), which shows at once whether the virtual MAC a client is told about is the one that answers.", "The buffer holds the newest 5000 packets, or 32 MB. **Download .tgz** saves it as an archive with one `.pcap` in it, for Wireshark or tcpdump (through another node, the newest part that fits about 5 MB). **Clear** empties the list, **Stop** ends the capture (what was captured stays until you start again). A node has one capture: starting another replaces it."]],
      "Cluster or gateway: the same capture runs on each node at the same time, for the time chosen, and the .tgz holds one .pcap per node. Each node keeps its newest packets (about 4 MB). This does not touch what anyone has on a node's own Capture page.",
      ["h", "The filter"],
      "The filter is a small version of the tcpdump language, applied on the node before a packet is kept: `host 10.0.0.5`, `src host`, `dst host`, `net 10.0.0.0/24`, `port 123`, `src port`, `dst port`, `portrange 50-60`, `tcp`, `udp`, `icmp`, `icmp6`, `arp`, `ip`, `ip6`, `ntp` (port 123), `ether host 00:1a:7c:01:02:00` (also `src`, `dst`), joined with `and`, `or`, `not` and brackets. A protocol before host, net or port narrows it (`tcp port 123`). A mistake is reported when you press Start. Names are not looked up: give addresses. An empty filter keeps everything, which on a busy NTP server fills the buffer in moments, so filter.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Interface", "The network card this node uses for the gateway (default `eth0`). This node only."], ["Filter", "Optional, up to 300 characters, as above. Press Enter to start."], ["How long", "Cluster or gateway only: 5, 10 (default), 30 or 60 seconds."]]],
      ["cli", "netdir --capture-interfaces\nnetdir --capture eth0 --capture-seconds 10 --capture-filter \"host 10.20.0.205 and port 123\"\nnetdir --capture eth0 --capture-file /tmp/eth0.pcap\nnetdir --capture eth0 --capture-seconds 30 --all-nodes --capture-file /tmp/cluster.tgz\nnetdir --capture - --group 100 --capture-seconds 30 --capture-file /tmp/gateway.tgz   (one gateway: its nodes only, its interface, only its addresses; --capture IFACE instead of - picks the interface)\n(without --capture-file the packets are printed, the first 500; with --all-nodes or --group a file is needed)"],
    ],
  },
  mcpusage: {
    title: "Monitor: MCP",
    body: [
      "Who uses the MCP endpoint, and for what, on the node this page is acting on (use the **Node** menu to pick another member: each node counts the calls it answers). The daemon counts every tool call by the **key** that made it (or *(address list)* when the caller got in by its address), the **source address**, the **tool** and the **device, tag or target** it asked about, and keeps the numbers by the hour for 8 days, so they survive a restart and an upgrade. Nothing a caller sent or was answered is kept, only the names, sizes and times.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["See how many calls were made in the last hour, 24 hours or 7 days, how many failed, how many were refused, and how many changes were made.", "See which key, which address, which tool and which device account for the calls.", "Read the newest 200 calls, one line each, to see what an assistant just did."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: find out who is hitting a device"],
      ["ol", ["Choose a **time range**.", "Read the tiles for the totals, then look at **By device** for the device's name (a call for `all` or `tag:site=dc1` counts for each device written as the target, and the tag or `all` is listed as it was written).", "Look at **By key** and **By source address** to see who asked, and **Newest calls** for the last 200 calls with their results."]],
      ["h", "Reading the tables"],
      ["ul", ["**Calls**: tool calls. **Errors**: the tool answered with an error. **Refused**: a read-only caller asked for a change. **Changes**: calls that change something and succeeded.", "**Data**: the size of the answers handed to the caller after privacy masking. **Avg ms**: the time a call took on this node.", "A call counts once under its key, once under its source, once under its tool and once under each device named in its target."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Time range", "The buttons **Last Hour**, **Last Day** (default) and **Last Week**. The charts are per hour, so the last hour shows one bar."], ["Tiles", "Calls, errors, refused changes, changes made, data returned and average time for the time range."]]],
      ["cli", "GET /api/mcpusage?window=24h   (the same numbers as JSON: by_key, by_source, by_tool, by_target, hours, recent; also the MCP tool api_get with that path)"],
    ],
  },
  log: {
    title: "Monitor: Log",
    body: [
      "The daemon's log on the node this page is acting on (use the **Node** menu to pick another member). It is the same text that goes to the system journal, and it is also kept in a file in the state directory (`netdir.log`, rotated at 100 MB with the two previous files kept), so it survives a restart.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Search the log for a word, an address or a name.", "Show only warnings and errors.", "Follow the log live.", "Download what you see, or a full **tshoot** bundle for support."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: find what happened to one server"],
      ["ol", ["Type the address in **Filter text**, for example `10.0.0.5 DOWN`. A line must contain every word.", "Choose a **Time range** and, if you like, a **Level**.", "Tick **Live** to follow new lines as they arrive."]],
      ["h", "Finding things"],
      ["ul", ["**Filter** takes words; a line must contain every one of them (any case); join words with `OR` to accept either, for example `error OR warn` (AND binds first). For example `10.0.0.5 DOWN` or `server 10.0.0.123`.", "**Level** keeps lines at that level and above. Debug lines exist only while the daemon's log level is *debug* (Configure ▸ General).", "**Time range** and **lines** bound the answer; the status line under the list says when older lines were cut.", "**Live** refreshes every few seconds and follows the newest line while you are at the bottom; scroll up to read and it stops following. **Download** saves what is shown as a text file."]],
      ["h", "tshoot"],
      "**tshoot** downloads one .tgz with everything that helps to find out why something does not work, from **every node** of the cluster (the node you are signed in to asks the others, whichever node the Node menu has picked): each node's log (the newest 20000 lines), configuration and its saved versions, cluster view, gateway, BGP, anycast, virtual-MAC-test and update state, host numbers for the last hour, the addresses, links, routes (every table), rules, neighbors, ARP, listening sockets, the sysctl settings that matter to ARP and routing, the firewall rules, FRR's BGP and BFD output and configuration, service status, the system journal of MCP Network Director, the kernel log tail and the system's version and load. It always includes 8 seconds of ARP and neighbor-discovery traffic on each gateway interface and a dump of every goroutine of the daemon (what it is busy with, for a node that is up but stuck). Passwords, the gateway key, tokens, join codes and private keys are removed before anything is written, command lines of other programs are not included, and each node's README.txt lists the files and says what could not be collected. A node that cannot be reached has a note instead of a folder. Nothing is changed on any node.",
      ["h", "What is logged"],
      "Server up and down changes, gateway start-up and hand-over, a node's CPU, memory or fullest disk going over 85% (a warning, naming what is over; this node and every cluster member) and the line when it is back at 85% or below, configuration changes (each one says what was enabled, disabled, paused or resumed), updates (including auto-update turned on or off), BGP and web sign-in events.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Filter text", "Words, separated by spaces. A line is shown only if it contains every one, in any case. Applied as you type."], ["Level", "All levels, Info and above, Warnings and errors, or Errors only. Default all."], ["Time range", "The buttons **Last hour**, **Last day** and **Last week** choose how much history the graphs show. They are per window; the history kept is 7 days."], ["Lines", "The most lines shown: 200, 1000 (default) or 5000, newest kept."], ["Live", "Ticked (the default): the list refreshes every few seconds and follows the newest line while you are at the bottom."]]],
      ["cli", "netdir --tshoot --all-nodes   (the tshoot bundle of every node, written to netdir-tshoot-HOST-TIME.tgz; without --all-nodes just this node; --tshoot-file F.tgz names the file; the ARP / neighbor-discovery capture is always taken)\nkill -USR1 $(pidof netdir)   (writes every goroutine's stack to the journal: journalctl -u netdir; for a daemon that is up but not answering)\nnetdir --log\nnetdir --log --log-min warn --log-since 6h\nnetdir --log --log-grep \"server 192.0.2.53\" --log-lines 200\n(without --log-lines the whole matching log is printed; redirect it to a file to keep it)"],
    ],
  },
  _default: {
    title: "Help",
    body: ["No help has been written for this page yet."],
  },
};
